<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document" style="font-size:207%;">Contextures: Representations from Contexts</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Runtian Zhai
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Carnegie Mellon University
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_email"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Email: </span><a href="mailto:"><span id="id1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">rzhai@alumni.cmu.edu</span></a>
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Kai Yang
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Peking University
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_email"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Email: </span><a href="mailto:"><span id="id2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">pradeepr@cs.cmu.edu</span></a>
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Che-Ping Tsai
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Carnegie Mellon University
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Burak Varıcı
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Carnegie Mellon University
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Zico Kolter
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Carnegie Mellon University
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Pradeep Ravikumar
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Carnegie Mellon University
</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Despite the empirical success of foundation models, we do not have a systematic characterization of the <em id="abstract1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">representations</em> that these models learn.
In this paper, we establish the contexture theory.
It shows that a large class of representation learning methods can be characterized as learning from the association between the input and a <em id="abstract1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">context variable</em>. Specifically, we show that many popular methods aim to approximate the top-<math id="abstract1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> singular functions of the expectation operator induced by the context, in which case we say that the representation <span id="abstract1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">learns the contexture</span>.
We demonstrate the generality of the contexture theory by proving that representation learning within various learning paradigms—supervised, self-supervised, and manifold learning—can all be studied from such a perspective.
We also prove that the representations that learn the contexture are optimal on those tasks that are compatible with the context.
One important implication of the contexture theory is that once the model is large enough to approximate the top singular functions, further scaling up the model size yields diminishing returns.
Therefore, scaling is not all we need, and further improvement requires better contexts.
To this end, we study how to evaluate the usefulness of a context without knowing the downstream tasks. We propose a metric and show by experiments that it correlates well with the actual performance of the encoder on many real datasets.</p>
  
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Representation learning underpins the modern deep learning revolution, leading up to the remarkable recent successes of foundation models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx5" title="" class="ltx_ref">BHA+21</a>]</cite>.
But a critical question that has remained unanswered to a satisfactory extent is: why are these models learning anything useful, or perhaps even what representations are these models learning? Unlike classical statistical learning theory, where there is no mystery regarding the statistical estimand, the very target itself is unclear in representation learning.
For example, what are the representations that BERT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx16" title="" class="ltx_ref">DCLT19</a>]</cite>—trained to do cloze tests—is learning, and why are they useful in understanding the sentiment of user reviews on Netflix? What representations do deep neural networks learn, and why are they useful if they cause neural collapse <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx43" title="" class="ltx_ref">PHD20</a>]</cite>, where deep representations could collapse to a few clusters? Do the many different self-supervised learning methods <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx7" title="" class="ltx_ref">BIS+23</a>]</cite> all learn similar or disparate representations?</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The responses to these questions are often muddled.
Many analyses are conflated with the mystery of deep learning generalization—the ability of large neural networks to learn function approximations that generalize to unseen points.
However, this is a very different problem from the mechanism of representation learning.
Our focus is on what representation learning (also known as “pretraining” in the context of foundation models) aims to capture, and why it can be applied to tasks completely different from the objectives used to train the representations.
Another way this question is muddled is by recourse to scaling.
A popular viewpoint argues that even if the encoder performs poorly on one task, increasing the model size while keeping everything else the same could allow better performance to “emerge” <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx58" title="" class="ltx_ref">WTB+22</a>]</cite>.
However, substantial evidence suggests that certain abilities cannot emerge solely from scaling.
Additional training signals, such as alignment <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx41" title="" class="ltx_ref">OWJ+22</a>]</cite>, are necessary.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The above questions are naturally interesting to learning theorists, but why should the broader machine learning community care about understanding the mechanism of representation learning, if empirical success seems to be always achievable with existing approaches by scaling up the model size, an empirical observation known as <span id="S1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">scaling laws</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx31" title="" class="ltx_ref">KMH+20</a>]</cite>?
This is because sustainable success or progress is not always guaranteed. Ilya Sutskever recently remarked that “pretraining as we know it will end” <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx51" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sut24</a>]</cite>, largely because the current pretraining paradigm is producing diminishing returns.
Understanding what representations are learned by foundation models is crucial for designing future generations of pretraining methods, and this is how this field can make scientific progress.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">In this work, we establish <span id="S1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">the contexture theory</span>, which provides a unified lens for inspecting a large class of representation learning methods.
The central argument of the contexture theory is that representations are learned from the association between the input <math id="S1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> and a context variable <math id="S1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>.
This framework is general enough to encompass a wide variety of learning paradigms, as we demonstrate in <a href="#S3" title="3 Learning the Contexture ‣ Contextures: Representations from Contexts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Now, suppose we are given a context variable <math id="S1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> along with <math id="S1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>, how should we learn the representation?
In <a href="#S4" title="4 Optimality of the Contexture ‣ Contextures: Representations from Contexts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> we prove that the optimal method is to approximate the top singular functions of the expectation operator induced by the context, in which case we say that the encoder “<span id="S1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">learns the contexture</span>”.
Such an encoder is optimal as long as the task is known to be <span id="S1.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">compatible</span> with the context, and in <a href="#S4" title="4 Optimality of the Contexture ‣ Contextures: Representations from Contexts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> we define a quantitative measurement of such compatibility.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Our theory implies that the main consequence of making the model large is that the learned representation space will be brought closer to the space spanned by the top-<math id="S1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> singular functions of the expectation operator, which we empirically verify in <a href="#S4.SS2" title="4.2 Implications for Scaling Laws ‣ 4 Optimality of the Contexture ‣ Contextures: Representations from Contexts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a>.
Once the two spaces are close enough, further scaling will yield diminishing returns.
We envision that future breakthroughs in pretraining require <span id="S1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">context scaling</span>, where better contexts are learned from data, instead of heuristics.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Taking a first step towards context scaling, in <a href="#S5" title="5 Context Evaluation ‣ Contextures: Representations from Contexts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> we study how to evaluate contexts.
This is a prerequisite because if we cannot even determine which contexts are good, then there will be no way to create better contexts.
The key takeaway from our analysis is that for a context to be useful (meaning that it can lead to good representations), the <span id="S1.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">association</span> between <math id="S1.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> should be moderate—neither too strong nor too weak.
For example, if <math id="S1.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A=X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A=X</annotation></semantics></math>, then their association is the strongest; if <math id="S1.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> is independent of <math id="S1.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>, then their association is the weakest. However, neither context is useful because <math id="S1.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> does not provide additional information about <math id="S1.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>.
In <a href="#S5.SS2" title="5.2 Task-agnostic Evaluation of Contexts ‣ 5 Context Evaluation ‣ Contextures: Representations from Contexts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.2</span></a>, we propose a quantitative measurement of context usefulness that can be efficiently estimated and does not require knowledge of the downstream task.
We also demonstrate empirically that the proposed metric correlates with the performance of the encoder on many real datasets.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p8.1" class="ltx_p">In one sentence, the key contribution of this work is clarity on the target of a large class of representation learning methods—the singular functions of the expectation operator. However, we do not discuss the numerical aspect of approximating these functions, which requires an expressive model architecture and a good optimizer, and such analyses are left to future work.</p>
</div>
<section id="S1.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">1.1 </span>Examples of Representation Learning Methods</h3>

<div id="S1.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Supervised learning is the simplest way to learn representations.
For example, the representations of neural networks pretrained on ImageNet <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx44" title="" class="ltx_ref">RDS+15</a>]</cite> were very popular in the early days of the deep learning boom <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx21" title="" class="ltx_ref">HAE16</a>]</cite>.
Specifically, one uses the output of an intermediate layer, typically the one before the last linear layer, as the representation. However, it has never been fully explained why the penultimate layer works so well on tasks very different from the pretraining task.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Self-supervised learning (SSL) is currently the most common way of learning representations.
Two of the most widely used SSL categories are multi-view learning and denoising autoencoders. Multi-view learning includes contrastive learning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx40" title="" class="ltx_ref">OLV18</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx14" title="" class="ltx_ref">CKNH20</a>]</cite> and non-contrastive learning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx19" title="" class="ltx_ref">GSA+20</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx65" title="" class="ltx_ref">ZJM+21</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx11" title="" class="ltx_ref">BPL22</a>]</cite>.
Denoising autoencoders have wide applications, including language <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx16" title="" class="ltx_ref">DCLT19</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx46" title="" class="ltx_ref">RWC+19</a>]</cite>, vision <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx24" title="" class="ltx_ref">HCX+22</a>]</cite>, videos <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx20" title="" class="ltx_ref">GWDL23</a>]</cite>, and more.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Manifold learning is a classical method that aims to capture the geometry of the data.
Examples such as locally linear embedding (LLE) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx45" title="" class="ltx_ref">RS00</a>]</cite> and Laplacian eigenmaps <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx10" title="" class="ltx_ref">BN03</a>]</cite> formulate manifold learning as node representation learning on a graph, where connected nodes should have similar embeddings.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">1.2 </span>Related Work</h3>

<div id="S1.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Understanding what representations an encoder learns has long been a hot research topic in machine learning.
In the pre-deep-learning-boom era, early work on manifold learning revealed the connection between representation learning and approximating the top eigenfunctions of a kernel <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx10" title="" class="ltx_ref">BN03</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx4" title="" class="ltx_ref">BDR+04</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx15" title="" class="ltx_ref">CL06</a>]</cite>.
Moreover, using the eigenvectors of the graph Laplacian as node representations on graphs was a classical technique in graph applications <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx9" title="" class="ltx_ref">BN02</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx64" title="" class="ltx_ref">ZGL03</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Although deep learning has achieved phenomenal success, it is not as theoretically driven as traditional methods, which makes it difficult to analyze what representations it learns.
Most early work experimentally studied the representations of neural networks with visualization tools <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx52" title="" class="ltx_ref">SVZ14</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx63" title="" class="ltx_ref">ZF14</a>]</cite>, or data manipulation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx62" title="" class="ltx_ref">ZBH+17</a>]</cite>.
These experimental tools are still widely used today to analyze large language models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx60" title="" class="ltx_ref">YN22</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx37" title="" class="ltx_ref">NCL+23</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx38" title="" class="ltx_ref">NLW23</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">On the theoretical understanding of representations, two lines of work are closely related to this paper.
The first line studies representation alignment <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx32" title="" class="ltx_ref">KNLH19</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx23" title="" class="ltx_ref">HCWI24</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx18" title="" class="ltx_ref">FPM+24</a>]</cite>.
These papers mainly focus on comparing between two representations, while this work aims to evaluate a single representation and the context in which it is trained.
Representation similarity has also been studied in neuroscience <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx30" title="" class="ltx_ref">KMB08</a>]</cite>.
The second line develops the spectral theory of self-supervised learning (SSL).
SSL has achieved remarkable success in recent years <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx46" title="" class="ltx_ref">RWC+19</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx14" title="" class="ltx_ref">CKNH20</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx65" title="" class="ltx_ref">ZJM+21</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx11" title="" class="ltx_ref">BPL22</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx24" title="" class="ltx_ref">HCX+22</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx6" title="" class="ltx_ref">BHX+22</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx39" title="" class="ltx_ref">ODM+23</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx1" title="" class="ltx_ref">ADM+23</a>]</cite>.
See the SSL cookbook by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx7" title="" class="ltx_ref">BIS+23</a>]</cite> for a summary of these methods.
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx25" title="" class="ltx_ref">HWGM21</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx26" title="" class="ltx_ref">JHM23</a>]</cite> related contrastive learning to the spectrum of the augmentation graph and the positive-pair kernel.
Then, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx66" title="" class="ltx_ref">ZLR+24</a>]</cite> extended the spectral theory to all SSL methods, not just contrastive learning.
This paper extends the spectral theory to an even broader scope than <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx66" title="" class="ltx_ref">ZLR+24</a>]</cite>.
Other theoretical work on SSL studies its training dynamics <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx17" title="" class="ltx_ref">DLS22</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx27" title="" class="ltx_ref">JVLT22</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx54" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tia22</a>]</cite> and builds its connection to information theory <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx2" title="" class="ltx_ref">AS18</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx8" title="" class="ltx_ref">BL22</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx53" title="" class="ltx_ref">SZBK+23</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Another line of work for characterizing the representations aims to learn disentangled or causally associated representations <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx22" title="" class="ltx_ref">HAP+18</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx48" title="" class="ltx_ref">SLB+21</a>]</cite>. It is shown that such representations can be provably recovered, provided sufficient variables are present in the environments, either through auxiliary variables or interventions <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx29" title="" class="ltx_ref">KKMH20</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx56" title="" class="ltx_ref">VAST24</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx12" title="" class="ltx_ref">BRR+23</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx61" title="" class="ltx_ref">YXL+24</a>]</cite>. Some of these results further require stringent parametric assumptions.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Prior work has developed quite different theoretical frameworks for different pretraining methods, and each framework is not applicable to other settings.
In this work, we provide a unified framework that spans the works discussed above and more—manifold learning, SSL based on augmentation, supervised learning, graph representation learning, etc.
It further extends the spectral theory in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx66" title="" class="ltx_ref">ZLR+24</a>]</cite> to other paradigms beyond SSL.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Definitions and Examples</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{X}}</annotation></semantics></math> be the <span id="S2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">input space</span>.
The goal of representation learning is to learn a feature encoder <math id="S2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi:{\mathcal{X}}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi:{\mathcal{X}}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>. We call <math id="S2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi(x)</annotation></semantics></math> the <span id="S2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">embedding</span> of <math id="S2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> the embedding dimension.
Let <math id="S2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{P_{\mathcal{X}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{P_{\mathcal{X}}}</annotation></semantics></math> be the data distribution.
Throughout this work, we assume <math id="S2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{P_{\mathcal{X}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{P_{\mathcal{X}}}</annotation></semantics></math> to be fixed.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The central argument of the contexture theory is that representations are learned from the association between the input <math id="S2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\in{\mathcal{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\in{\mathcal{X}}</annotation></semantics></math> and a context variable <math id="S2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A\in{\mathcal{A}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A\in{\mathcal{A}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
<math id="S2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{A}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{A}}</annotation></semantics></math> is called the context space.
Let <math id="S2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{+}(x,a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{+}(x,a)</annotation></semantics></math> be the joint distribution of <math id="S2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>, with marginal distributions <math id="S2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{P_{\mathcal{X}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{P_{\mathcal{X}}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{P_{\mathcal{A}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{P_{\mathcal{A}}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let <math id="S2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{L^{2}(P_{\mathcal{X}})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{L^{2}(P_{\mathcal{X}})}</annotation></semantics></math> be the <math id="S2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> function space <span id="S2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">w.r.t.</span> <math id="S2.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{P_{\mathcal{X}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{P_{\mathcal{X}}}</annotation></semantics></math>, with inner product <math id="S2.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\left\langle f_{1},f_{2}\right\rangle}_{P_{\mathcal{X}}}=\mathbb{E}_{X\sim{P_{\mathcal{X}}}}[f_{1}(X)f_{2}(X)]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo>⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>⟩</mo></mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msub></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\left\langle f_{1},f_{2}\right\rangle}_{P_{\mathcal{X}}}=\mathbb{E}_{X\sim{P_{\mathcal{X}}}}[f_{1}(X)f_{2}(X)]</annotation></semantics></math> and norm <math id="S2.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\left\|f\right\|}_{P_{\mathcal{X}}}=\sqrt{{\left\langle f,f\right\rangle}_{P_{\mathcal{X}}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>‖</mo></mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msub></msub><mo>=</mo><msqrt><msub><mrow><mo>⟨</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo>⟩</mo></mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msub></msub></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\left\|f\right\|}_{P_{\mathcal{X}}}=\sqrt{{\left\langle f,f\right\rangle}_{P_{\mathcal{X}}}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Define <math id="S2.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{L^{2}(P_{\mathcal{A}})},{\left\langle\cdot,\cdot\right\rangle}_{{P_{\mathcal{A}}}},{\left\|\cdot\right\|}_{{P_{\mathcal{A}}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mrow><mo>⟨</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo></mrow><mo>⟩</mo></mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></msub></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>‖</mo></mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></msub></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{L^{2}(P_{\mathcal{A}})},{\left\langle\cdot,\cdot\right\rangle}_{{P_{\mathcal{A}}}},{\left\|\cdot\right\|}_{{P_{\mathcal{A}}}}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S2.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{P_{\mathcal{A}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{P_{\mathcal{A}}}</annotation></semantics></math> similarly.</p>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1 </span>Examples of Contexts</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We first introduce some commonly used contexts, their corresponding <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{+}</annotation></semantics></math>, and how <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{+}</annotation></semantics></math> is provided in practice.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<ol id="S2.I1" class="ltx_enumerate ltx_leftmargin_flush">
<li id="S2.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Labels</span> are a common type of context in machine learning. They can take different forms, such as discrete categories in classification, continuous values in regression, or structured outputs like text sequences in image captioning. Labels may be obtained from human annotators or in pseudo-forms, such as clusters or teacher models. Typically, labels are provided as compatible pairs sampled from the joint distribution <math id="S2.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{+}(x,a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{+}(x,a)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Random transformations</span> generate different views of the same data point. Common transformations include adding random noise to inputs, as seen in diffusion models and denoising autoencoders, or randomly corrupting/masking inputs, as in SimCLR and masked autoencoder. These transformations are typically defined by domain experts and are specified through a predefined conditional distribution <math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{+}{\left(a\;\middle|\;x\right)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="true">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{+}{\left(a\;\middle|\;x\right)}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Graphs</span> provide locality information about the inputs. The edge values are typically given, which represent the similarity between two inputs.
We can have a continuous space version of such a graph kernel to capture local geometry, which in the limit can be related to differential operators such as the Laplace-Beltrami operator on a manifold. In this case, we have <math id="S2.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{A}}={\mathcal{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>=</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{A}}={\mathcal{X}}</annotation></semantics></math> with the conditional distribution <math id="S2.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{+}{\left(a\;\middle|\;x\right)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="true">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{+}{\left(a\;\middle|\;x\right)}</annotation></semantics></math> proportional to the edge values between <math id="S2.I1.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.I1.i3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math>. Please refer to <a href="#S3.SS3" title="3.3 Node Representation Learning: Graph Context ‣ 3 Learning the Contexture ‣ Contextures: Representations from Contexts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a> for a more detailed construction of <math id="S2.I1.i3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{+}{\left(a\;\middle|\;x\right)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="true">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{+}{\left(a\;\middle|\;x\right)}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">4.</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I1.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Stochastic Features</span> are potentially stochastic predefined or pretrained mappings from <math id="S2.I1.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{X}}</annotation></semantics></math> to a vector space, which we denote by <math id="S2.I1.i4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a=\Omega(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a=\Omega(x)</annotation></semantics></math>.
This encompasses teacher models that provide stochastic pretrained feature encoders. In contrast to previous instances, here <math id="S2.I1.i4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{+}{\left(a\;\middle|\;x\right)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="true">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{+}{\left(a\;\middle|\;x\right)}</annotation></semantics></math> is directly described via a reparameterization or structural equation for <math id="S2.I1.i4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> in terms of <math id="S2.I1.i4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2 </span>Induced Kernels and Expectation Operator</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The joint distribution <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{+}</annotation></semantics></math> fully determines the association between <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>.
It induces the positive-pair kernel <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx26" title="" class="ltx_ref">JHM23</a>]</cite> and the dual kernel <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx66" title="" class="ltx_ref">ZLR+24</a>]</cite> defined as follows.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 1</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The <span id="Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">positive-pair kernel</span> <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{k_{A}^{+}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>A</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{k_{A}^{+}}</annotation></semantics></math> and its <span id="Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">dual kernel</span> <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{k_{X}^{+}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{k_{X}^{+}}</annotation></semantics></math> are defined as</span></p>
<table id="S2.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.Ex1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle{k_{A}^{+}}(a,a^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>A</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>a</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle{k_{A}^{+}}(a,a^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\frac{P^{+}(a,a^{\prime})}{{P_{\mathcal{A}}}(a){P_{\mathcal{A}}}(a^{\prime})}=\frac{\int P^{+}{\left(a\;\middle|\;x\right)}P^{+}{\left(a^{\prime}\;\middle|\;x\right)}d{P_{\mathcal{X}}}(x)}{{P_{\mathcal{A}}}(a){P_{\mathcal{A}}}(a^{\prime})};" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>a</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>a</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="true">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>a</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" stretchy="true">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>a</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo>;</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\frac{P^{+}(a,a^{\prime})}{{P_{\mathcal{A}}}(a){P_{\mathcal{A}}}(a^{\prime})}=\frac{\int P^{+}{\left(a\;\middle|\;x\right)}P^{+}{\left(a^{\prime}\;\middle|\;x\right)}d{P_{\mathcal{X}}}(x)}{{P_{\mathcal{A}}}(a){P_{\mathcal{A}}}(a^{\prime})};</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S2.Ex2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle{k_{X}^{+}}(x,x^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle{k_{X}^{+}}(x,x^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\frac{P^{+}(x,x^{\prime})}{{P_{\mathcal{X}}}(x){P_{\mathcal{X}}}(x^{\prime})}=\frac{\int P^{+}{\left(x\;\middle|\;a\right)}P^{+}{\left(x^{\prime}\;\middle|\;a\right)}d{P_{\mathcal{A}}}(a)}{{P_{\mathcal{X}}}(x){P_{\mathcal{X}}}(x^{\prime})}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="true">|</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" stretchy="true">|</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\frac{P^{+}(x,x^{\prime})}{{P_{\mathcal{X}}}(x){P_{\mathcal{X}}}(x^{\prime})}=\frac{\int P^{+}{\left(x\;\middle|\;a\right)}P^{+}{\left(x^{\prime}\;\middle|\;a\right)}d{P_{\mathcal{A}}}(a)}{{P_{\mathcal{X}}}(x){P_{\mathcal{X}}}(x^{\prime})}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">These two kernels are density ratios between joints and marginals for <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively. The kernels captures how more likely
<math id="S2.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(a,a^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>a</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(a,a^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x,x^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x,x^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> appear together than independently given <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{+}</annotation></semantics></math>.
<math id="S2.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{+}</annotation></semantics></math> also induces the following expectation operator. Intuitively, given a function <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\in{L^{2}(P_{\mathcal{A}})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\in{L^{2}(P_{\mathcal{A}})}</annotation></semantics></math>, the expectation operator computes the expectation of <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(A)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(A)</annotation></semantics></math> conditioned on any given <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 2</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The <span id="Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">expectation operator</span> <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{P^{+}}:{L^{2}(P_{\mathcal{A}})}\rightarrow{L^{2}(P_{\mathcal{X}})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{P^{+}}:{L^{2}(P_{\mathcal{A}})}\rightarrow{L^{2}(P_{\mathcal{X}})}</annotation></semantics></math> is defined as for all <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\in{L^{2}(P_{\mathcal{A}})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\in{L^{2}(P_{\mathcal{A}})}</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\left(T_{P^{+}}g\right)}(x)=\int g(a)P^{+}{\left(a\;\middle|\;x\right)}da=\mathbb{E}{\left[g(A)\;\middle|\;x\right]}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="true">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="true">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\left(T_{P^{+}}g\right)}(x)=\int g(a)P^{+}{\left(a\;\middle|\;x\right)}da=\mathbb{E}{\left[g(A)\;\middle|\;x\right]}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmtheorem2.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Its adjoint operator <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{P^{+}}^{*}:{L^{2}(P_{\mathcal{X}})}\rightarrow{L^{2}(P_{\mathcal{A}})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>T</mi><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{P^{+}}^{*}:{L^{2}(P_{\mathcal{X}})}\rightarrow{L^{2}(P_{\mathcal{A}})}</annotation></semantics></math>, which satisfies <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\langle f,T_{P^{+}}g\rangle_{{P_{\mathcal{X}}}}=\langle T_{P^{+}}^{*}f,g\rangle_{{P_{\mathcal{A}}}}\;(\forall f\in{L^{2}(P_{\mathcal{X}})},g\in{L^{2}(P_{\mathcal{A}})})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></msub><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msub></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><msubsup><mi>T</mi><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></msub></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle f,T_{P^{+}}g\rangle_{{P_{\mathcal{X}}}}=\langle T_{P^{+}}^{*}f,g\rangle_{{P_{\mathcal{A}}}}\;(\forall f\in{L^{2}(P_{\mathcal{X}})},g\in{L^{2}(P_{\mathcal{A}})})</annotation></semantics></math>, is given by <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\left(T_{P^{+}}^{*}f\right)}(a)=\int f(x)\frac{P^{+}{\left(a\;\middle|\;x\right)}{P_{\mathcal{X}}}(x)}{{P_{\mathcal{A}}}(a)}dx=\mathbb{E}{\left[f(X)\;\middle|\;a\right]}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>T</mi><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="true">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="true">|</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\left(T_{P^{+}}^{*}f\right)}(a)=\int f(x)\frac{P^{+}{\left(a\;\middle|\;x\right)}{P_{\mathcal{X}}}(x)}{{P_{\mathcal{A}}}(a)}dx=\mathbb{E}{\left[f(X)\;\middle|\;a\right]}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Now we discuss the spectral properties of these operators.
Define the kernel integral operator <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{{k_{A}^{+}}}:{L^{2}(P_{\mathcal{A}})}\rightarrow{L^{2}(P_{\mathcal{A}})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>A</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{{k_{A}^{+}}}:{L^{2}(P_{\mathcal{A}})}\rightarrow{L^{2}(P_{\mathcal{A}})}</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(T_{{k_{A}^{+}}}g)(a)=\int g(a^{\prime}){k_{A}^{+}}(a,a^{\prime})d{P_{\mathcal{A}}}(a^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>A</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>a</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>A</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>a</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>a</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(T_{{k_{A}^{+}}}g)(a)=\int g(a^{\prime}){k_{A}^{+}}(a,a^{\prime})d{P_{\mathcal{A}}}(a^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math>.
Define the other operator <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{{k_{X}^{+}}}:{L^{2}(P_{\mathcal{X}})}\rightarrow{L^{2}(P_{\mathcal{X}})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{{k_{X}^{+}}}:{L^{2}(P_{\mathcal{X}})}\rightarrow{L^{2}(P_{\mathcal{X}})}</annotation></semantics></math> similarly.
It is easy to see that <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{{k_{A}^{+}}}=T_{P^{+}}^{*}T_{P^{+}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>A</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>T</mi><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{{k_{A}^{+}}}=T_{P^{+}}^{*}T_{P^{+}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{{k_{X}^{+}}}=T_{P^{+}}T_{P^{+}}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>T</mi><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{{k_{X}^{+}}}=T_{P^{+}}T_{P^{+}}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">We call <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda\in\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda\in\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> an eigenvalue of <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{{k_{A}^{+}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>A</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{{k_{A}^{+}}}</annotation></semantics></math> with eigenfunction <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu\in{L^{2}(P_{\mathcal{A}})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ν</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu\in{L^{2}(P_{\mathcal{A}})}</annotation></semantics></math>, if <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{{k_{A}^{+}}}\nu=\lambda\nu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>A</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ν</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ν</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{{k_{A}^{+}}}\nu=\lambda\nu</annotation></semantics></math>.
Suppose <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{{k_{A}^{+}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>A</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{{k_{A}^{+}}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a Hilbert-Schmidt integral operator. Then, we can order its eigenvalues by <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1=\lambda_{0}\geq\lambda_{1}\geq\cdots\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.1389em">≥</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.1389em">⋯</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em">≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1=\lambda_{0}\geq\lambda_{1}\geq\cdots\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>, and the corresponding eigenfunctions <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu_{0},\nu_{1},\cdots" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em">⋯</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu_{0},\nu_{1},\cdots</annotation></semantics></math> form an orthonormal basis (ONB) of <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{L^{2}(P_{\mathcal{A}})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{L^{2}(P_{\mathcal{A}})}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Here <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{i}\leq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{i}\leq 1</annotation></semantics></math> because of Jensen’s inequality,
and <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu_{0}\equiv 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>≡</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu_{0}\equiv 1</annotation></semantics></math> is always an eigenfunction of <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{{k_{A}^{+}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>A</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{{k_{A}^{+}}}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{0}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{0}=1</annotation></semantics></math>.
Similarly, we can order the eigenvalues of <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{{k_{X}^{+}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{{k_{X}^{+}}}</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1=\kappa_{0}\geq\kappa_{1}\geq\cdots\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.1389em">≥</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.1389em">⋯</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em">≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1=\kappa_{0}\geq\kappa_{1}\geq\cdots\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>, and the eigenfunctions <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{0},\mu_{1},\cdots" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em">⋯</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{0},\mu_{1},\cdots</annotation></semantics></math> form an ONB of <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{L^{2}(P_{\mathcal{X}})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{L^{2}(P_{\mathcal{X}})}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{0}\equiv 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>≡</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{0}\equiv 1</annotation></semantics></math>.
We also have the following result.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 3</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Duality property, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx66" title="" class="ltx_ref">ZLR+24</a>]</cite>, Proposition 1)<span id="Thmtheorem3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For all <math id="Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>, we have <math id="Thmtheorem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{i}=\kappa_{i}\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>κ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{i}=\kappa_{i}\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>. And if <math id="Thmtheorem3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{i}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{i}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, then we have <math id="Thmtheorem3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{i}=\lambda_{i}^{-\frac{1}{2}}T_{P^{+}}\nu_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>λ</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ν</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{i}=\lambda_{i}^{-\frac{1}{2}}T_{P^{+}}\nu_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="Thmtheorem3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu_{i}=\lambda_{i}^{-\frac{1}{2}}T_{P^{+}}^{*}\mu_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>λ</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>T</mi><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu_{i}=\lambda_{i}^{-\frac{1}{2}}T_{P^{+}}^{*}\mu_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">We call <math id="S2.SS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{i}=\lambda_{i}^{\frac{1}{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>λ</mi><mi>i</mi><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{i}=\lambda_{i}^{\frac{1}{2}}</annotation></semantics></math> a <span id="S2.SS2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">singular value</span> of <math id="S2.SS2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{P^{+}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{P^{+}}</annotation></semantics></math>, associated with left <span id="S2.SS2.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">singular function</span> <math id="S2.SS2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{i}\in{L^{2}(P_{\mathcal{X}})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{i}\in{L^{2}(P_{\mathcal{X}})}</annotation></semantics></math> and right singular function <math id="S2.SS2.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu_{i}\in{L^{2}(P_{\mathcal{A}})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu_{i}\in{L^{2}(P_{\mathcal{A}})}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Since we choose <math id="S2.SS2.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{0}\equiv 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>≡</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{0}\equiv 1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu_{0}\equiv 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>≡</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu_{0}\equiv 1</annotation></semantics></math>, all other <math id="S2.SS2.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> (and <math id="S2.SS2.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ν</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>) must have zero mean because they are orthogonal to <math id="S2.SS2.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> (and <math id="S2.SS2.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>).
Using these singular functions, we can spectrally decompose <math id="S2.SS2.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{+}</annotation></semantics></math> as follows.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 4</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The spectral decomposition of <math id="Thmtheorem4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P+" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P+</annotation></semantics></math> is
<math id="Thmtheorem4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{+}(x,a)=\sum_{i}s_{i}\mu_{i}(x)\nu_{i}(a){P_{\mathcal{X}}}(x){P_{\mathcal{A}}}(a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ν</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{+}(x,a)=\sum_{i}s_{i}\mu_{i}(x)\nu_{i}(a){P_{\mathcal{X}}}(x){P_{\mathcal{A}}}(a)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S2.SS2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S2.SS2.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\forall i,\;{\left\langle\frac{P^{+}(x,a)}{{P_{\mathcal{X}}}(x){P_{\mathcal{A}}}(a)},\nu_{i}\right\rangle}_{{P_{\mathcal{A}}}}=\int P^{+}{\left(a\;\middle|\;x\right)}\nu_{i}(a)da=(T_{P^{+}}\nu_{i})(x)={\left(\lambda_{i}^{\frac{1}{2}}\mu_{i}\right)}(x)=s_{i}\mu_{i}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mo>⟨</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ν</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>⟩</mo></mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></msub></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="true">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ν</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ν</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>λ</mi><mi>i</mi><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\forall i,\;{\left\langle\frac{P^{+}(x,a)}{{P_{\mathcal{X}}}(x){P_{\mathcal{A}}}(a)},\nu_{i}\right\rangle}_{{P_{\mathcal{A}}}}=\int P^{+}{\left(a\;\middle|\;x\right)}\nu_{i}(a)da=(T_{P^{+}}\nu_{i})(x)={\left(\lambda_{i}^{\frac{1}{2}}\mu_{i}\right)}(x)=s_{i}\mu_{i}(x)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.SS2.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">.
Since <math id="S2.SS2.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\nu_{i})_{i\geq 0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ν</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\nu_{i})_{i\geq 0}</annotation></semantics></math> is an ONB, we have <math id="S2.SS2.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{P^{+}(x,a)}{{P_{\mathcal{X}}}(x){P_{\mathcal{A}}}(a)}=\sum_{i=0}^{\infty}s_{i}\mu_{i}(x)\nu_{i}(a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msubsup><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ν</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{P^{+}(x,a)}{{P_{\mathcal{X}}}(x){P_{\mathcal{A}}}(a)}=\sum_{i=0}^{\infty}s_{i}\mu_{i}(x)\nu_{i}(a)</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p7.1" class="ltx_p">The first key result of the contexture theory is that the optimal <math id="S2.SS2.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional representation should recover the linear space spanned by the top-<math id="S2.SS2.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> singular functions <math id="S2.SS2.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{1},\cdots,\mu_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{1},\cdots,\mu_{d}</annotation></semantics></math>.
We say that such a representation learns the contexture.
Note that the constant function <math id="S2.SS2.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{0}\equiv 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>≡</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{0}\equiv 1</annotation></semantics></math> is excluded, as it does not need to be learned—there is no benefit in allocating a dimension to encode something already universally present.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 5</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">A <math id="Thmtheorem5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional encoder <math id="Thmtheorem5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi=[\phi_{1},\cdots,\phi_{d}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi=[\phi_{1},\cdots,\phi_{d}]</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="Thmtheorem5.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">learns the contexture</span> of <math id="Thmtheorem5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{+}</annotation></semantics></math>, if there exists a set of top-<math id="Thmtheorem5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> singular functions <math id="Thmtheorem5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\left\{\mu_{1},\cdots,\mu_{d}\right\}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>{</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\left\{\mu_{1},\cdots,\mu_{d}\right\}}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="Thmtheorem5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{P^{+}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{P^{+}}</annotation></semantics></math>, such that <math id="Thmtheorem5.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\textnormal{span}}{\left\{\phi_{1},\cdots,\phi_{d}\right\}}={\textnormal{span}}{\left\{\mu_{1},\cdots,\mu_{d}\right\}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext>span</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>span</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\textnormal{span}}{\left\{\phi_{1},\cdots,\phi_{d}\right\}}={\textnormal{span}}{\left\{\mu_{1},\cdots,\mu_{d}\right\}}</annotation></semantics></math>. We also say that <math id="Thmtheorem5.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="Thmtheorem5.p1.1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">extracts the top-<math id="Thmtheorem5.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> eigenspace</span> of <math id="Thmtheorem5.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{{k_{X}^{+}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{{k_{X}^{+}}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p8.1" class="ltx_p">If the multiplicity of <math id="S2.SS2.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> is more than <math id="S2.SS2.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S2.SS2.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math> recovering the span of any top-<math id="S2.SS2.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> singular functions suffices.
The intuition why learning the contexture is ideal is that such a representation keeps the most information (variance) of the context, which is analogous to principal component analysis (PCA) in the finite-dimensional case.
Consider the case where <math id="S2.SS2.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{X}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{A}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{A}}</annotation></semantics></math> are both finite sets.
Let <math id="S2.SS2.p8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=|{\mathcal{X}}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=|{\mathcal{X}}|</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.p8.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M=|{\mathcal{A}}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M=|{\mathcal{A}}|</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then, a function <math id="S2.SS2.p8.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\in{L^{2}(P_{\mathcal{X}})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\in{L^{2}(P_{\mathcal{X}})}</annotation></semantics></math> is a vector in <math id="S2.SS2.p8.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>N</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{N}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS2.p8.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\in{L^{2}(P_{\mathcal{A}})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\in{L^{2}(P_{\mathcal{A}})}</annotation></semantics></math> is a vector in <math id="S2.SS2.p8.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>M</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{M}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S2.SS2.p8.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{P^{+}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{P^{+}}</annotation></semantics></math> is essentially a matrix <math id="S2.SS2.p8.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{T}}\in\mathbb{R}^{N\times M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝑻</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{T}}\in\mathbb{R}^{N\times M}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Suppose we want to learn a <math id="S2.SS2.p8.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional embedding <math id="S2.SS2.p8.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{E}}\in\mathbb{R}^{N\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝑬</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{E}}\in\mathbb{R}^{N\times d}</annotation></semantics></math> for the <math id="S2.SS2.p8.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> samples in <math id="S2.SS2.p8.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{X}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and it should preserve the information of <math id="S2.SS2.p8.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{T}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝑻</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{T}}</annotation></semantics></math> as much as possible, then what should we do?
PCA states that we should use the top-<math id="S2.SS2.p8.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> left singular vectors of <math id="S2.SS2.p8.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{T}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝑻</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{T}}</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="S2.SS2.p8.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{E}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝑬</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{E}}</annotation></semantics></math>, which are equivalent to the top-<math id="S2.SS2.p8.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> eigenvectors of <math id="S2.SS2.p8.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{T}}{\bm{T}}^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝑻</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝑻</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{T}}{\bm{T}}^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math>, because they maximize the explained variance.
Similarly, functional spaces are essentially infinite-dimensional vector spaces, so the <math id="S2.SS2.p8.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional embedding of <math id="S2.SS2.p8.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> that preserves the most information of <math id="S2.SS2.p8.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{P^{+}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{P^{+}}</annotation></semantics></math> consists of the top-<math id="S2.SS2.p8.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> left singular functions of <math id="S2.SS2.p8.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{P^{+}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{P^{+}}</annotation></semantics></math>, or equivalently the top-<math id="S2.SS2.p8.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> eigenfunctions of <math id="S2.SS2.p8.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{{k_{X}^{+}}}=T_{P^{+}}T_{P^{+}}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>T</mi><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{{k_{X}^{+}}}=T_{P^{+}}T_{P^{+}}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Learning the Contexture</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we show that every example method in <a href="#S1.SS1" title="1.1 Examples of Representation Learning Methods ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Contextures: Representations from Contexts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a> does one of the following:</p>
<ol id="S3.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S3.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(i)</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Extracts the top-<math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> eigenspace of <math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{{k_{X}^{+}}}=T_{P^{+}}T_{P^{+}}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>T</mi><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{{k_{X}^{+}}}=T_{P^{+}}T_{P^{+}}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> (learns the contexture of <math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{+}</annotation></semantics></math>), which according to <a href="#Thmtheorem5" title="Definition 5. ‣ 2.2 Induced Kernels and Expectation Operator ‣ 2 Definitions and Examples ‣ Contextures: Representations from Contexts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Definition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> is equivalent to recovering the span of <math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{1},\cdots,\mu_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{1},\cdots,\mu_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> (excluding <math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{0}\equiv 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>≡</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{0}\equiv 1</annotation></semantics></math>);</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(ii)</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Extracts the top-<math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> eigenspace of <math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{P^{+}}\Lambda T_{P^{+}}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>T</mi><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{P^{+}}\Lambda T_{P^{+}}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda</annotation></semantics></math> is the integral operator of a kernel <math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{\Lambda}(a,a^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>a</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{\Lambda}(a,a^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math>, that is <math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\Lambda g)(a)=\int g(a^{\prime})k_{\Lambda}(a,a^{\prime})d{P_{\mathcal{A}}}(a^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>a</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>a</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>a</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\Lambda g)(a)=\int g(a^{\prime})k_{\Lambda}(a,a^{\prime})d{P_{\mathcal{A}}}(a^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math>. <math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{\Lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>k</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{\Lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> is called the <span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">loss kernel</span>, which is defined by the loss function used in the objective.
Since the constant function is not necessarily the top eigenfunction of <math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{P^{+}}\Lambda T_{P^{+}}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>T</mi><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{P^{+}}\Lambda T_{P^{+}}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>, in this case, we do not exclude any eigenfunction.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
<div id="S3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Notation:</span> For any <math id="S3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\in{L^{2}(P_{\mathcal{X}})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\in{L^{2}(P_{\mathcal{X}})}</annotation></semantics></math>, denote its mean by <math id="S3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{f}=\mathbb{E}_{{P_{\mathcal{X}}}}[f(X)]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{f}=\mathbb{E}_{{P_{\mathcal{X}}}}[f(X)]</annotation></semantics></math>, and its centered version by <math id="S3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{f}=f-\bar{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>−</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{f}=f-\bar{f}</annotation></semantics></math>. The same notation is used for multi-dimensional functions and random variables, as long as the distribution is clear from context.
The covariance matrix of any <math id="S3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi:{\mathcal{X}}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi:{\mathcal{X}}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>, denoted by <math id="S3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Cov}_{{P_{\mathcal{X}}}}[\Phi]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Cov</mi><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Cov}_{{P_{\mathcal{X}}}}[\Phi]</annotation></semantics></math>, is a <math id="S3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\times d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\times d</annotation></semantics></math> matrix <math id="S3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{C}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝑪</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{C}}</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S3.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{C}}[i,j]={\left\langle\tilde{\phi}_{i},\tilde{\phi}_{j}\right\rangle}_{{P_{\mathcal{X}}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝑪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mo>⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo>⟩</mo></mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msub></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{C}}[i,j]={\left\langle\tilde{\phi}_{i},\tilde{\phi}_{j}\right\rangle}_{{P_{\mathcal{X}}}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Supervised Learning: Label Context</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In the supervised learning paradigm, the context variable <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> is the label of <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="P^{+}{\left(\cdot\;\middle|\;x\right)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.224em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="true">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{+}{\left(\cdot\;\middle|\;x\right)}</annotation></semantics></math> is the label distribution of <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>.
Consider minimizing the mean squared error (MSE):</p>
<table id="S3.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{R}}(\Phi)=\min_{{\bm{W}}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{A}\times d},{\bm{b}}\in R^{d_{A}}}\mathbb{E}_{(X,A)\sim P^{+}}{\left[{\left\|{\bm{W}}\Phi(X)+{\bm{b}}-A\right\|}_{2}^{2}\right]}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><munder><mi>min</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>𝑾</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>𝒃</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></msup></mrow></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∼</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝑾</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>𝒃</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{R}}(\Phi)=\min_{{\bm{W}}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{A}\times d},{\bm{b}}\in R^{d_{A}}}\mathbb{E}_{(X,A)\sim P^{+}}{\left[{\left\|{\bm{W}}\Phi(X)+{\bm{b}}-A\right\|}_{2}^{2}\right]}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">That is, <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi(X)</annotation></semantics></math> is the output of the layer before the last linear layer in a neural network, and <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{b}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒃</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{b}}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the bias.
If <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{b}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒃</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{b}}</annotation></semantics></math> can be an arbitrary vector, then the linear layer is biased; if <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{b}}={\bm{0}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒃</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>𝟎</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{b}}={\bm{0}}</annotation></semantics></math> is fixed, then the linear layer is unbiased.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">First, we study classification tasks where <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> is one-hot.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 6</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Proof in <a href="#A1.SS1" title="A.1 Proof of Theorem ‣ Appendix A Proofs for Section ‣ Contextures: Representations from Contexts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.1</span></a>)<span id="Thmtheorem6.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmtheorem6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> be a one-hot random vector.
Suppose the linear layer is unbiased, that is <math id="Thmtheorem6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{b}}={\bm{0}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐛</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>𝟎</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{b}}={\bm{0}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then, <math id="Thmtheorem6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> minimizes <math id="Thmtheorem6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{R}}(\Phi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{R}}(\Phi)</annotation></semantics></math> if and only if it extracts the top-<math id="Thmtheorem6.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> eigenspace of <math id="Thmtheorem6.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{P^{+}}\Lambda T_{P^{+}}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>T</mi><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{P^{+}}\Lambda T_{P^{+}}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="Thmtheorem6.p1.m7" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="k_{\Lambda}(a,a^{\prime})={\mathbb{I}}[a=a^{\prime}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>a</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>𝕀</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>a</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{\Lambda}(a,a^{\prime})={\mathbb{I}}[a=a^{\prime}]</annotation></semantics></math>, or <math id="Thmtheorem6.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\Lambda g)(a)=g(a){P_{\mathcal{A}}}(a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\Lambda g)(a)=g(a){P_{\mathcal{A}}}(a)</annotation></semantics></math>.
If all classes have the same size, then the top-<math id="Thmtheorem6.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> eigenfunctions of <math id="Thmtheorem6.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{P^{+}}\Lambda T_{P^{+}}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>T</mi><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{P^{+}}\Lambda T_{P^{+}}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmtheorem6.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{P^{+}}T_{P^{+}}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>T</mi><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{P^{+}}T_{P^{+}}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> are the same.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">This theorem works for randomized labels, where each <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> could belong to multiple classes with certain probabilities.
The loss kernel <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{\Lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>k</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{\Lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> is a consequence of class imbalance, and it puts more weights on larger classes.
Indeed, in practice, smaller classes are harder to learn.
To get rid of <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda</annotation></semantics></math>, we can use the class-balanced risk (also known as importance weighting <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx47" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shi00</a>]</cite>):</p>
<table id="S3.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{R}}_{\rm bal}(\Phi)=\min_{{\bm{W}}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{A}\times d},{\bm{b}}\in R^{d_{A}}}\mathbb{E}_{(X,A)\sim P^{+}}{\left[\frac{{\left\|{\bm{W}}\Phi(X)+{\bm{b}}-A\right\|}_{2}^{2}}{\sqrt{{P_{\mathcal{A}}}(A)}}\right]}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>bal</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><munder><mi>min</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>𝑾</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>𝒃</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></msup></mrow></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∼</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mfrac><msubsup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝑾</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>𝒃</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><msqrt><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mfrac><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{R}}_{\rm bal}(\Phi)=\min_{{\bm{W}}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{A}\times d},{\bm{b}}\in R^{d_{A}}}\mathbb{E}_{(X,A)\sim P^{+}}{\left[\frac{{\left\|{\bm{W}}\Phi(X)+{\bm{b}}-A\right\|}_{2}^{2}}{\sqrt{{P_{\mathcal{A}}}(A)}}\right]}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem7" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 7</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Proof in <a href="#A1.SS2" title="A.2 Proof of Theorem ‣ Appendix A Proofs for Section ‣ Contextures: Representations from Contexts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.2</span></a>)<span id="Thmtheorem7.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem7.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Under the setting of <a href="#Thmtheorem6" title="Theorem 6 (Proof in ). ‣ 3.1 Supervised Learning: Label Context ‣ 3 Learning the Contexture ‣ Contextures: Representations from Contexts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>, suppose the linear layer is biased. Then, <math id="Thmtheorem7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> minimizes <math id="Thmtheorem7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{R}}_{\rm bal}(\Phi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>bal</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{R}}_{\rm bal}(\Phi)</annotation></semantics></math> if and only if it learns the contexture of <math id="Thmtheorem7.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{+}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Interestingly, our result can partially explain <span id="S3.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">neural collapse</span>.
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx43" title="" class="ltx_ref">PHD20</a>]</cite> empirically showed that when there are <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> classes of equal sizes and the label <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> is deterministic, a sufficiently trained deep representation will collapse to an equiangular tight frame (ETF) <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{1},\cdots,\phi_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{1},\cdots,\phi_{d}</annotation></semantics></math>, which are defined as <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{i}(x)=c({\mathbb{I}}[x\text{ belongs to class }i]-d^{-1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝕀</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> belongs to class </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{i}(x)=c({\mathbb{I}}[x\text{ belongs to class }i]-d^{-1})</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in[d]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in[d]</annotation></semantics></math> and some constant <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math>.
The span of <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{1},\cdots,\phi_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{1},\cdots,\phi_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> is the same as the span of the top-<math id="S3.SS1.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> eigenfunctions of <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{P^{+}}\Lambda T_{P^{+}}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>T</mi><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{P^{+}}\Lambda T_{P^{+}}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>.
However, our result cannot explain why <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{1},\cdots,\phi_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{1},\cdots,\phi_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> converge to the exact functions as above—it only proves that they will span the same space.
To explain this, one needs to analyze the training dynamics, which depend on the specific optimizer, such as gradient-based methods, while our results are independent of the optimizer.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">When the classes have different sizes, it is easy to see that the dual kernel of <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{P^{+}}\Lambda T_{P^{+}}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>T</mi><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{P^{+}}\Lambda T_{P^{+}}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{k_{X}^{+}}(x,x^{\prime})={\mathbb{I}}[x\text{ and }x^{\prime}\text{ have the same label}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝕀</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> and </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> have the same label</mtext></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{k_{X}^{+}}(x,x^{\prime})={\mathbb{I}}[x\text{ and }x^{\prime}\text{ have the same label}]</annotation></semantics></math>. This is equivalent to the simplex-encoded labels interpolation (SELI) defined by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx55" title="" class="ltx_ref">TKVB22</a>, Definition 2]</cite>, which generalizes neural collapse.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">For regression where <math id="S3.SS1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> is an arbitrary Euclidean vector, using the same objective as <a href="#S3.E1" title="In 3.1 Supervised Learning: Label Context ‣ 3 Learning the Contexture ‣ Contextures: Representations from Contexts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Equation</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, we can prove the following result.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem8" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem8.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 8</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem8.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Proof in <a href="#A1.SS3" title="A.3 Proof of Theorem ‣ Appendix A Proofs for Section ‣ Contextures: Representations from Contexts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.3</span></a>)<span id="Thmtheorem8.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem8.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="Thmtheorem8.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi^{*}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="Thmtheorem8.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> minimizes <a href="#S3.E1" title="In 3.1 Supervised Learning: Label Context ‣ 3 Learning the Contexture ‣ Contextures: Representations from Contexts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Equation</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> if and only if <math id="Thmtheorem8.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> extracts the top-<math id="Thmtheorem8.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> eigenspace of <math id="Thmtheorem8.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{P^{+}}\Lambda T_{P^{+}}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>T</mi><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{P^{+}}\Lambda T_{P^{+}}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>.
If the linear layer is unbiased (<math id="Thmtheorem8.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{b}}={\bm{0}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐛</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>𝟎</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{b}}={\bm{0}}</annotation></semantics></math>), then <math id="Thmtheorem8.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{\Lambda}(a,a^{\prime})={\left\langle a,a^{\prime}\right\rangle}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>a</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>⟨</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>a</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{\Lambda}(a,a^{\prime})={\left\langle a,a^{\prime}\right\rangle}</annotation></semantics></math>;
if it is biased (<math id="Thmtheorem8.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{b}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐛</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{b}}</annotation></semantics></math> can be arbitrary), then <math id="Thmtheorem8.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{\Lambda}(a,a^{\prime})={\left\langle\tilde{a},\tilde{a^{\prime}}\right\rangle}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>a</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>⟨</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>a</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><msup><mi>a</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><mo>⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{\Lambda}(a,a^{\prime})={\left\langle\tilde{a},\tilde{a^{\prime}}\right\rangle}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmremarkx1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmremarkx1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark</span></span><span id="Thmremarkx1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmremarkx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmremarkx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmremarkx1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Kernel <math id="Thmremarkx1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{\Lambda}(a,a^{\prime})={\left\langle a,a^{\prime}\right\rangle}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>a</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>⟨</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>a</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{\Lambda}(a,a^{\prime})={\left\langle a,a^{\prime}\right\rangle}</annotation></semantics></math> is called the <span id="Thmremarkx1.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">linear kernel</span> on <math id="Thmremarkx1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{A}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{A}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="Thmremarkx1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{\Lambda}(a,a^{\prime})={\left\langle\tilde{a},\tilde{a^{\prime}}\right\rangle}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>a</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>⟨</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>a</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><msup><mi>a</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><mo>⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{\Lambda}(a,a^{\prime})={\left\langle\tilde{a},\tilde{a^{\prime}}\right\rangle}</annotation></semantics></math> is called the <span id="Thmremarkx1.p1.1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">centered linear kernel</span> w.r.t. <math id="Thmremarkx1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{P_{\mathcal{A}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{P_{\mathcal{A}}}</annotation></semantics></math>. <a href="#Thmtheorem6" title="Theorem 6 (Proof in ). ‣ 3.1 Supervised Learning: Label Context ‣ 3 Learning the Contexture ‣ Contextures: Representations from Contexts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> is a special case of <a href="#Thmtheorem8" title="Theorem 8 (Proof in ). ‣ 3.1 Supervised Learning: Label Context ‣ 3 Learning the Contexture ‣ Contextures: Representations from Contexts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Self-supervised Learning (SSL): Transformation Context</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Two major types of SSL are multi-view learning and denoising autoencoders.
Multi-view learning independently samples two views <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A,A^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A,A^{+}</annotation></semantics></math> from <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="P^{+}(\cdot|x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{+}(\cdot|x)</annotation></semantics></math> for every <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>. <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{+}</annotation></semantics></math> is called a positive sample of <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then, one trains <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Psi:{\mathcal{A}}\to\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi:{\mathcal{A}}\to\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Psi(A)\approx\Psi(A^{+})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≈</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi(A)\approx\Psi(A^{+})</annotation></semantics></math>.
This <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi</annotation></semantics></math> is an encoder on <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{A}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{A}}</annotation></semantics></math> instead of <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{X}}</annotation></semantics></math>, so at downstream we need to convert <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Psi(a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi(a)</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi(x)</annotation></semantics></math>, which is typically done via the <span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">average encoder</span>:</p>
<table id="S3.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi(x)=(T_{P^{+}}\Psi)(x)=\int\Psi(a)dP^{+}{\left(a\;\middle|\;x\right)}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="true">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi(x)=(T_{P^{+}}\Psi)(x)=\int\Psi(a)dP^{+}{\left(a\;\middle|\;x\right)}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">By <a href="#Thmtheorem3" title="Lemma 3 (Duality property, [], Proposition 1). ‣ 2.2 Induced Kernels and Expectation Operator ‣ 2 Definitions and Examples ‣ Contextures: Representations from Contexts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Lemma</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, we have the following corollary.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem9" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_corollary">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem9.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 9</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem9.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem9.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem9.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem9.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmtheorem9.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{d}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{d}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>. The average encoder <math id="Thmtheorem9.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math> spanning the span of the left top-<math id="Thmtheorem9.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> singular functions of <math id="Thmtheorem9.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{P^{+}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{P^{+}}</annotation></semantics></math> is equivalent to <math id="Thmtheorem9.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi</annotation></semantics></math> spanning the span of the right top-<math id="Thmtheorem9.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> singular functions of <math id="Thmtheorem9.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{P^{+}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{P^{+}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Enforcing <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Psi(A)\approx\Psi(A^{+})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≈</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi(A)\approx\Psi(A^{+})</annotation></semantics></math> alone leads to the degenerate solution where <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi</annotation></semantics></math> gives the same embedding to all <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math>.
This is called the <span id="S3.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">feature collapse</span> problem.
There are two solutions: contrastive learning and non-contrastive learning.
Prior work by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx25" title="" class="ltx_ref">HWGM21</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx26" title="" class="ltx_ref">JHM23</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx66" title="" class="ltx_ref">ZLR+24</a>]</cite> showed that the following spectral contrastive loss <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{L}}_{\rm{C}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{L}}_{\rm{C}}</annotation></semantics></math> and spectral non-contrastive loss <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{L}}_{\rm{N}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{L}}_{\rm{N}}</annotation></semantics></math> can learn the contexture of <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{+}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{+}</annotation></semantics></math> be a positive sample of <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> drawn from the same <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{-}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>−</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{-}</annotation></semantics></math> be a negative sample drawn from another <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> independently.</p>
<table id="S3.EGx2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex6"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle{\mathcal{L}}_{\rm{C}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle{\mathcal{L}}_{\rm{C}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\mathbb{E}{\left[-{\left\langle\tilde{\Psi}(A),\tilde{\Psi}(A^{+})\right\rangle}+\frac{1}{2}{\left\langle\tilde{\Psi}(A),\tilde{\Psi}(A^{-})\right\rangle}^{2}\right]};" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo>⟨</mo><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo>⟨</mo><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>−</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>⟩</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>;</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\mathbb{E}{\left[-{\left\langle\tilde{\Psi}(A),\tilde{\Psi}(A^{+})\right\rangle}+\frac{1}{2}{\left\langle\tilde{\Psi}(A),\tilde{\Psi}(A^{-})\right\rangle}^{2}\right]};</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.Ex7"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle{\mathcal{L}}_{\rm{N}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle{\mathcal{L}}_{\rm{N}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\mathbb{E}{\left[{\left\|\Psi(A)-\Psi(A^{+})\right\|}_{2}^{2}\right]}\;\;\text{s.t.}\;\;\mathrm{Cov}_{{P_{\mathcal{A}}}}{\left[\Psi\right]}={\bm{I}}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.560em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>s.t.</mtext><mo lspace="0.560em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Cov</mi><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>𝑰</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\mathbb{E}{\left[{\left\|\Psi(A)-\Psi(A^{+})\right\|}_{2}^{2}\right]}\;\;\text{s.t.}\;\;\mathrm{Cov}_{{P_{\mathcal{A}}}}{\left[\Psi\right]}={\bm{I}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where the <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(i,j)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(i,j)</annotation></semantics></math>-th entry of <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Cov}_{{P_{\mathcal{A}}}}{\left[\Psi\right]}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Cov</mi><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Cov}_{{P_{\mathcal{A}}}}{\left[\Psi\right]}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\left\langle\psi_{i},\psi_{j}\right\rangle}_{{P_{\mathcal{A}}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo>⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo>⟩</mo></mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\left\langle\psi_{i},\psi_{j}\right\rangle}_{{P_{\mathcal{A}}}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i,j\in[d]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i,j\in[d]</annotation></semantics></math>.
Minimizing <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{L}}_{\rm{N}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{L}}_{\rm{N}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a constrained optimization problem.
The constraint <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Cov}_{{P_{\mathcal{A}}}}{\left[\Psi\right]}={\bm{I}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Cov</mi><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>𝑰</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Cov}_{{P_{\mathcal{A}}}}{\left[\Psi\right]}={\bm{I}}</annotation></semantics></math> is called the <span id="S3.SS2.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">orthonormality constraint</span>.
It makes sure that <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi</annotation></semantics></math> must be rank-<math id="S3.SS2.p2.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>, so that it cannot be a constant function on <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{A}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{A}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem10" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem10.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 10</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem10.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Proof in <a href="#A1.SS4" title="A.4 Proof of Theorem ‣ Appendix A Proofs for Section ‣ Contextures: Representations from Contexts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.4</span></a>)<span id="Thmtheorem10.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem10.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem10.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="Thmtheorem10.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Psi^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi^{*}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="Thmtheorem10.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> minimizes <math id="Thmtheorem10.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{L}}_{\rm{C}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">C</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{L}}_{\rm{C}}</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="Thmtheorem10.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{L}}_{\rm{N}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">N</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{L}}_{\rm{N}}</annotation></semantics></math> if and only if <math id="Thmtheorem10.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\Phi}^{*}=T_{P^{+}}\tilde{\Psi}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\Phi}^{*}=T_{P^{+}}\tilde{\Psi}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> learns the contexture.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">For denoising autoencoders, suppose <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{X}}\subseteq\mathbb{R}^{d_{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>⊆</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>X</mi></msub></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{X}}\subseteq\mathbb{R}^{d_{X}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then, consider minimizing the following objective:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S3.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{R}}(\Psi)=\min_{{\bm{W}}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{X}\times d},\;{\bm{b}}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{X}}}\;\underset{(X,A)\sim P^{+}}{\mathbb{E}}\;{\left[{\left\|{\bm{W}}\Psi(A)+{\bm{b}}-X\right\|}_{2}^{2}\right]}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><munder><mi>min</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>𝑾</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><mrow><mi>𝒃</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>X</mi></msub></msup></mrow></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">(</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">A</mi><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">)</mo></mrow><mo mathsize="0.700em">∼</mo><msup><mi mathsize="0.700em">P</mi><mo mathsize="0.710em">+</mo></msup></mrow></munder></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝑾</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>𝒃</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{R}}(\Psi)=\min_{{\bm{W}}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{X}\times d},\;{\bm{b}}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{X}}}\;\underset{(X,A)\sim P^{+}}{\mathbb{E}}\;{\left[{\left\|{\bm{W}}\Psi(A)+{\bm{b}}-X\right\|}_{2}^{2}\right]}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem11" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem11.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 11</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem11.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem11.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem11.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem11.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmtheorem11.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Psi^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> be any minimizer of <a href="#S3.E2" title="In 3.2 Self-supervised Learning (SSL): Transformation Context ‣ 3 Learning the Contexture ‣ Contextures: Representations from Contexts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Equation</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>. Then, <math id="Thmtheorem11.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\Psi}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\Psi}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> extracts the top-<math id="Thmtheorem11.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> eigenspace of <math id="Thmtheorem11.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{P^{+}}^{*}\Lambda T_{P^{+}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>T</mi><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{P^{+}}^{*}\Lambda T_{P^{+}}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="Thmtheorem11.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda</annotation></semantics></math> is the integral operator of <math id="Thmtheorem11.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{\Lambda}(x,x^{\prime})={\left\langle\tilde{x},\tilde{x}^{\prime}\right\rangle}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>⟨</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{\Lambda}(x,x^{\prime})={\left\langle\tilde{x},\tilde{x}^{\prime}\right\rangle}</annotation></semantics></math> if <math id="Thmtheorem11.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{b}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐛</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{b}}</annotation></semantics></math> can be an arbitrary vector, or <math id="Thmtheorem11.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{\Lambda}(x,x^{\prime})={\left\langle x,x^{\prime}\right\rangle}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>⟨</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{\Lambda}(x,x^{\prime})={\left\langle x,x^{\prime}\right\rangle}</annotation></semantics></math> if <math id="Thmtheorem11.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{b}}={\bm{0}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐛</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>𝟎</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{b}}={\bm{0}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Consequently, <math id="Thmtheorem11.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\Phi}^{*}=T_{P^{+}}\tilde{\Psi}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\Phi}^{*}=T_{P^{+}}\tilde{\Psi}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> extracts the top-<math id="Thmtheorem11.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> eigenspace of <math id="Thmtheorem11.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{P^{+}}T_{P^{+}}^{*}\Lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>T</mi><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{P^{+}}T_{P^{+}}^{*}\Lambda</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">The proof is the same as <a href="#Thmtheorem8" title="Theorem 8 (Proof in ). ‣ 3.1 Supervised Learning: Label Context ‣ 3 Learning the Contexture ‣ Contextures: Representations from Contexts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3 </span>Node Representation Learning: Graph Context</h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{G}}=({\mathcal{V}},{\mathcal{E}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{G}}=({\mathcal{V}},{\mathcal{E}})</annotation></semantics></math> be an undirected graph, where edge <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(u,v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(u,v)</annotation></semantics></math> has a weight <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w(u,v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w(u,v)</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w(u,v)=w(v,u)\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w(u,v)=w(v,u)\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let the degree of node <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> be <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d(u)=\sum_{v\in{\mathcal{V}}}w(u,v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d(u)=\sum_{v\in{\mathcal{V}}}w(u,v)</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{\textrm{sum}}=\sum_{v}d(v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>sum</mtext></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>v</mi></msub><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{\textrm{sum}}=\sum_{v}d(v)</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{P_{\mathcal{X}}}(u)=\frac{d(u)}{d_{\textrm{sum}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>sum</mtext></msub></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{P_{\mathcal{X}}}(u)=\frac{d(u)}{d_{\textrm{sum}}}</annotation></semantics></math> be a distribution on <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{V}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{V}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{w}(u,v)=\frac{w(u,v)}{d_{\textrm{sum}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>sum</mtext></msub></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{w}(u,v)=\frac{w(u,v)}{d_{\textrm{sum}}}</annotation></semantics></math> be a distribution on <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{E}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{E}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Define <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{+}(v|u)=\frac{w(u,v)}{d(u)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{+}(v|u)=\frac{w(u,v)}{d(u)}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then, the following optimization problem with a similar orthonormality constraint can learn the contexture of <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{+}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
<table id="S3.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E3.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\underset{\Phi:{\mathcal{X}}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{d}}{\text{minimize}}\quad\frac{1}{2}\mathbb{E}_{(u,v)\sim P_{w}}{\left[{\left\|\Phi(u)-\Phi(v)\right\|}_{2}^{2}\right]}\qquad\text{s.t.}\qquad\mathrm{Cov}_{{P_{\mathcal{X}}}}[\Phi]={\bm{I}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mtext>minimize</mtext><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em" mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" mathsize="0.700em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.700em">𝒳</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em" stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi mathsize="0.700em">ℝ</mi><mi mathsize="0.710em">d</mi></msup></mrow></mrow></munder><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mrow><mo>[</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>]</mo></mrow><mtext>s.t.</mtext><msub><mi>Cov</mi><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msub></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>𝑰</mi><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underset{\Phi:{\mathcal{X}}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{d}}{\text{minimize}}\quad\frac{1}{2}\mathbb{E}_{(u,v)\sim P_{w}}{\left[{\left\|\Phi(u)-\Phi(v)\right\|}_{2}^{2}\right]}\qquad\text{s.t.}\qquad\mathrm{Cov}_{{P_{\mathcal{X}}}}[\Phi]={\bm{I}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem12" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem12.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 12</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem12.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Proof in <a href="#A1.SS5" title="A.5 Proof of Theorem ‣ Appendix A Proofs for Section ‣ Contextures: Representations from Contexts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.5</span></a>)<span id="Thmtheorem12.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem12.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem12.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem12.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmtheorem12.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> be any solution to <a href="#S3.E3" title="In 3.3 Node Representation Learning: Graph Context ‣ 3 Learning the Contexture ‣ Contextures: Representations from Contexts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Equation</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> (so that for any constant <math id="Thmtheorem12.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Thmtheorem12.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi^{*}+c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>+</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi^{*}+c</annotation></semantics></math> is also a solution).
Then, <math id="Thmtheorem12.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\Phi}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\Phi}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> learns the contexture of <math id="Thmtheorem12.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{+}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Optimality of the Contexture</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">So far, we have shown that commonly used learning objectives can learn the contexture.
Now, we present a rigorous theory that addresses the question of <em id="S4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">why</em> and <em id="S4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">when</em> learning the contexture is optimal. Arguably, no representations can be good for all downstream tasks. However, we show that a feature encoder that learns the contexture is optimal for the class of tasks that are <span id="S4.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">compatible</span> with the context. This provides a quantitative characterization of when a task respects the human prior knowledge the context incorporates.
Interestingly, as we detail in the sequel, this has intriguing implications for scaling laws.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Compatibility</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The ultimate evaluation of an encoder is its performance on relevant downstream tasks.
Most downstream tasks, such as prediction, clustering, and segmentation, can be associated with a target function <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{*}\in{L^{2}(P_{\mathcal{X}})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{*}\in{L^{2}(P_{\mathcal{X}})}</annotation></semantics></math>.
For example, multi-class classification can be associated with multiple one-vs-all labeling functions.
Moreover, if we are fitting a linear predictor on top of <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math>, then the mean and variance of <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> do not matter because we can change the weight and bias of the predictor accordingly.
Thus, we can assume that <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> is normalized, that is, it has zero mean and unit variance.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We say that a context <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{+}</annotation></semantics></math> is <span id="S4.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">useful</span> for a task <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>, if it can help us learn a good predictor for <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Formally, suppose <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="A\sim P^{+}{\left(\cdot\;\middle|\;x\right)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>∼</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.224em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="true">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A\sim P^{+}{\left(\cdot\;\middle|\;x\right)}</annotation></semantics></math> is a random corruption of <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>.
Consider the scenario where we have a corrupted training set <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\left\{(a_{i},y_{i})\right\}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\left\{(a_{i},y_{i})\right\}}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="a_{i}\sim P^{+}{\left(\cdot\;\middle|\;x_{i}\right)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.224em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="true">|</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{i}\sim P^{+}{\left(\cdot\;\middle|\;x_{i}\right)}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{i}=f^{*}(x_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{i}=f^{*}(x_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>.
That is, we cannot see the original samples <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, but can only see the corrupted samples <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>.
To learn a predictor on this training set, we can train a predictor <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{g}:{\mathcal{A}}\rightarrow{\mathcal{Y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>g</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{g}:{\mathcal{A}}\rightarrow{\mathcal{Y}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and then use <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{f}=\mathbb{E}{\left[\hat{g}(A)\;\middle|\;x\right]}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>g</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="true">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{f}=\mathbb{E}{\left[\hat{g}(A)\;\middle|\;x\right]}</annotation></semantics></math>.
At test time, given input <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>, we can draw <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m14" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="a\sim P^{+}{\left(\cdot\;\middle|\;x\right)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∼</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.224em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="true">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\sim P^{+}{\left(\cdot\;\middle|\;x\right)}</annotation></semantics></math> and then output the average of <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g^{*}(a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g^{*}(a)</annotation></semantics></math>.
For this procedure to work, two conditions are necessary:</p>
<ul id="S4.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S4.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">There exists <math id="S4.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g^{*}\in{L^{2}(P_{\mathcal{A}})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g^{*}\in{L^{2}(P_{\mathcal{A}})}</annotation></semantics></math> s.t. <math id="S4.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{*}(x)=\mathbb{E}{\left[g^{*}(A)\;\middle|\;x\right]}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="true">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{*}(x)=\mathbb{E}{\left[g^{*}(A)\;\middle|\;x\right]}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This <math id="S4.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> has a low <math id="S4.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Var}{\left[g^{*}(A)\;\middle|\;x\right]}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Var</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="true">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Var}{\left[g^{*}(A)\;\middle|\;x\right]}</annotation></semantics></math> on average over <math id="S4.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">If <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Var}{\left[g^{*}(A)\;\middle|\;x\right]}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Var</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="true">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Var}{\left[g^{*}(A)\;\middle|\;x\right]}</annotation></semantics></math> is high, then <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g^{*}(a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g^{*}(a)</annotation></semantics></math> will be far away from <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y=f^{*}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y=f^{*}(x)</annotation></semantics></math>, so fitting <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>g</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{g}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(a,y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(a,y)</annotation></semantics></math> will not work.
Based on these insights, we define compatibility as follows.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem13" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem13.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 13</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem13.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem13.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem13.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem13.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The <span id="Thmtheorem13.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">compatibility</span> with <math id="Thmtheorem13.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{+}</annotation></semantics></math> of any non-zero <math id="Thmtheorem13.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\in{L^{2}(P_{\mathcal{X}})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\in{L^{2}(P_{\mathcal{X}})}</annotation></semantics></math> is defined as</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho(f,P^{+})=\max_{g\in{L^{2}(P_{\mathcal{A}})},g\neq{\bm{0}}}\frac{{\left\langle\tilde{f},T_{P^{+}}g\right\rangle}_{{P_{\mathcal{X}}}}}{{\left\|\tilde{f}\right\|}_{{P_{\mathcal{X}}}}{\left\|g\right\|}_{{P_{\mathcal{A}}}}}\in[0,1]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><munder><mi>max</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≠</mo><mn>𝟎</mn></mrow></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mfrac><msub><mrow><mo>⟨</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>⟩</mo></mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msub></msub><mrow><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>‖</mo></mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>‖</mo></mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></msub></msub></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho(f,P^{+})=\max_{g\in{L^{2}(P_{\mathcal{A}})},g\neq{\bm{0}}}\frac{{\left\langle\tilde{f},T_{P^{+}}g\right\rangle}_{{P_{\mathcal{X}}}}}{{\left\|\tilde{f}\right\|}_{{P_{\mathcal{X}}}}{\left\|g\right\|}_{{P_{\mathcal{A}}}}}\in[0,1].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">For further insight, let <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f=\sum_{i}u_{i}\mu_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f=\sum_{i}u_{i}\mu_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=\sum_{i}v_{i}\nu_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ν</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=\sum_{i}v_{i}\nu_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then, <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho(f,P^{+})=\underset{v_{i}}{\max}\frac{\sum_{i\geq 1}s_{i}u_{i}v_{i}}{\sqrt{\sum_{i\geq 1}u_{i}^{2}}\sqrt{\sum_{i}v_{i}^{2}}}=\sqrt{\frac{\sum_{i\geq 1}s_{i}^{2}u_{i}^{2}}{\sum_{i\geq 1}u_{i}^{2}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>max</mi><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">v</mi><mi mathsize="0.710em">i</mi></msub></munder><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><msub><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em" stretchy="true">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mstyle><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><msub><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em" stretchy="true">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mstyle><msubsup><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></msqrt><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><msub><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em" stretchy="true">∑</mo><mi>i</mi></msub></mstyle><msubsup><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mfrac><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><msub><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em" stretchy="true">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mstyle><mrow><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><msub><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em" stretchy="true">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mstyle><msubsup><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mfrac></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho(f,P^{+})=\underset{v_{i}}{\max}\frac{\sum_{i\geq 1}s_{i}u_{i}v_{i}}{\sqrt{\sum_{i\geq 1}u_{i}^{2}}\sqrt{\sum_{i}v_{i}^{2}}}=\sqrt{\frac{\sum_{i\geq 1}s_{i}^{2}u_{i}^{2}}{\sum_{i\geq 1}u_{i}^{2}}}</annotation></semantics></math> by Cauchy-Schwarz inequality (the optimal <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfy <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{0}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{0}=0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{i}\propto s_{i}u_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∝</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{i}\propto s_{i}u_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>).
For any <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, we define the class of <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1-\epsilon)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1-\epsilon)</annotation></semantics></math>-compatible tasks as</p>
<table id="S4.Ex9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{\mathcal{F}}_{\epsilon}(P^{+})={\left\{f\in{L^{2}(P_{\mathcal{X}})}:\mathbb{E}[f]=0,\rho(f,P^{+})\geq 1-\epsilon\right\}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>ϵ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>}</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{F}}_{\epsilon}(P^{+})={\left\{f\in{L^{2}(P_{\mathcal{X}})}:\mathbb{E}[f]=0,\rho(f,P^{+})\geq 1-\epsilon\right\}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.2" class="ltx_p">This class of tasks satisfies the two conditions, <span id="S4.SS1.p3.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">i.e.</span> we can find a <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> with low variance <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Var}{\left[g^{*}(A)\;\middle|\;x\right]}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Var</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="true">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Var}{\left[g^{*}(A)\;\middle|\;x\right]}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem14" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem14.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 14</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem14.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Proof in <a href="#A2.SS1" title="B.1 Proof of Theorem ‣ Appendix B Proofs for Section ‣ Contextures: Representations from Contexts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.1</span></a>)<span id="Thmtheorem14.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem14.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem14.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem14.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For any <math id="Thmtheorem14.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{*}\in{\mathcal{F}}_{\epsilon}(P^{+})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>ϵ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{*}\in{\mathcal{F}}_{\epsilon}(P^{+})</annotation></semantics></math>, there exists a <math id="Thmtheorem14.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g^{*}\in{L^{2}(P_{\mathcal{A}})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g^{*}\in{L^{2}(P_{\mathcal{A}})}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="Thmtheorem14.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{*}(x)=\mathbb{E}{\left[g^{*}(A)\;\middle|\;x\right]}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="true">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{*}(x)=\mathbb{E}{\left[g^{*}(A)\;\middle|\;x\right]}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="Thmtheorem14.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfies</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underset{X\sim{P_{\mathcal{X}}}}{\mathbb{E}}\;\underset{A,A^{\prime}\sim P^{+}{\left(\cdot\;\middle|\;X\right)}}{\mathbb{E}}{\left[{\left(g^{*}(A)-g^{*}(A^{\prime})\right)}^{2}\right]}\leq 4\epsilon{\left\|g^{*}\right\|}_{{P_{\mathcal{A}}}}^{2}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">X</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">∼</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.710em">𝒳</mi></msub></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.280em" rspace="0em">​</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">A</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">,</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">A</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo mathsize="0.710em">′</mo></msup><mo mathsize="0.700em">∼</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">P</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo mathsize="0.710em">+</mo></msup><mo>(</mo><mo lspace="0em" mathsize="0.700em" rspace="0.224em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="true">|</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">X</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>‖</mo></mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></msub><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underset{X\sim{P_{\mathcal{X}}}}{\mathbb{E}}\;\underset{A,A^{\prime}\sim P^{+}{\left(\cdot\;\middle|\;X\right)}}{\mathbb{E}}{\left[{\left(g^{*}(A)-g^{*}(A^{\prime})\right)}^{2}\right]}\leq 4\epsilon{\left\|g^{*}\right\|}_{{P_{\mathcal{A}}}}^{2}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Now that we have a class of tasks compatible with <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{+}</annotation></semantics></math>, we evaluate <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math> by its worst-case approximation error on <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{F}}_{\epsilon}(P^{+})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>ϵ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{F}}_{\epsilon}(P^{+})</annotation></semantics></math>.
The most common way to evaluate <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math> is to fit a linear predictor on top, also called a <span id="S4.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">linear probe</span>, which is the focus of our attention (other methods for using <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math> include fitting a small neural network on top, using a kernel method, or using KNN).
Specifically, the worst-case approximation error of <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{F}}\subset{L^{2}(P_{\mathcal{X}})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{F}}\subset{L^{2}(P_{\mathcal{X}})}</annotation></semantics></math> is the maximum error of the optimal linear probe in estimating any function in <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{F}}</annotation></semantics></math>. In this work, we focus on the <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> error.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem15" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem15.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 15</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem15.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem15.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem15.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem15.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmtheorem15.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{F}}\subset{L^{2}(P_{\mathcal{X}})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{F}}\subset{L^{2}(P_{\mathcal{X}})}</annotation></semantics></math> be a function class where <math id="Thmtheorem15.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\in{\mathcal{F}}\Rightarrow\alpha f\in{\mathcal{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo stretchy="false">⇒</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\in{\mathcal{F}}\Rightarrow\alpha f\in{\mathcal{F}}</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="Thmtheorem15.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\in\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\in\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The <span id="Thmtheorem15.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">worst-case approximation error</span> of <math id="Thmtheorem15.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi:{\mathcal{X}}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi:{\mathcal{X}}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="Thmtheorem15.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{F}}</annotation></semantics></math> is defined as</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textnormal{err}{\left(\Phi;{\mathcal{F}}\right)}=\max_{f\in{\mathcal{F}}(P^{+}),\;{\left\|f\right\|}_{{P_{\mathcal{X}}}}=1}\;\textnormal{err}{\left(\Phi,f\right)},\quad\text{where}\quad\textnormal{err}{\left(\Phi,f\right)}=\min_{{\bm{w}}\in\mathbb{R}^{d},\;b\in\mathbb{R}}\;{\left\|{\bm{w}}^{\top}\Phi+b-f\right\|}_{P_{\mathcal{X}}}^{2}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>err</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><munder><mi>max</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>‖</mo></mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msub></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mtext>err</mtext></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">where</mtext><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><mtext>err</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><munder><mi>min</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>𝒘</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow></munder><mo>⁡</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>𝒘</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msub><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textnormal{err}{\left(\Phi;{\mathcal{F}}\right)}=\max_{f\in{\mathcal{F}}(P^{+}),\;{\left\|f\right\|}_{{P_{\mathcal{X}}}}=1}\;\textnormal{err}{\left(\Phi,f\right)},\quad\text{where}\quad\textnormal{err}{\left(\Phi,f\right)}=\min_{{\bm{w}}\in\mathbb{R}^{d},\;b\in\mathbb{R}}\;{\left\|{\bm{w}}^{\top}\Phi+b-f\right\|}_{P_{\mathcal{X}}}^{2}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">The following key result shows that the <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math> that minimizes <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textnormal{err}(\Phi;{\mathcal{F}}_{\epsilon}(P^{+}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>err</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>ϵ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textnormal{err}(\Phi;{\mathcal{F}}_{\epsilon}(P^{+}))</annotation></semantics></math> over all <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional encoders must recover the linear space spanned by the <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{1},\cdots,\mu_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{1},\cdots,\mu_{d}</annotation></semantics></math>. Here <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> is excluded since the bias <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math> in the linear predictor implicitly contains <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem16" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem16.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 16</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem16.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Proof in <a href="#A2.SS2" title="B.2 Proof of Theorem ‣ Appendix B Proofs for Section ‣ Contextures: Representations from Contexts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.2</span></a>)<span id="Thmtheorem16.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem16.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem16.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem16.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Suppose <math id="Thmtheorem16.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1-s_{1}\leq{\epsilon}\leq 1-\sqrt{\frac{s_{1}^{2}+s_{2}^{2}}{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msqrt><mfrac><mrow><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></msqrt></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1-s_{1}\leq{\epsilon}\leq 1-\sqrt{\frac{s_{1}^{2}+s_{2}^{2}}{2}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
For any <math id="Thmtheorem16.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>, among all <math id="Thmtheorem16.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi=[\phi_{1},\cdots,\phi_{d}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi=[\phi_{1},\cdots,\phi_{d}]</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="Thmtheorem16.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{i}\in{L^{2}(P_{\mathcal{X}})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{i}\in{L^{2}(P_{\mathcal{X}})}</annotation></semantics></math> , <math id="Thmtheorem16.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math> minimizes <math id="Thmtheorem16.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textnormal{err}(\Phi;{\mathcal{F}}_{\epsilon}(P^{+}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>err</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>ϵ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textnormal{err}(\Phi;{\mathcal{F}}_{\epsilon}(P^{+}))</annotation></semantics></math> if and only if it learns the contexture of <math id="Thmtheorem16.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{P^{+}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{P^{+}}</annotation></semantics></math>. The error is given by</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex12.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\min_{\Phi:{\mathcal{X}}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{d},\;\phi_{i}\in{L^{2}(P_{\mathcal{X}})}}\;\textnormal{err}{\left(\Phi;{\mathcal{F}}_{\epsilon}(P^{+})\right)}=\frac{s_{1}^{2}-(1-{\epsilon})^{2}}{s_{1}^{2}-s_{d+1}^{2}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder><mi>min</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></munder><mtext>err</mtext><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>;</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>ϵ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\min_{\Phi:{\mathcal{X}}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{d},\;\phi_{i}\in{L^{2}(P_{\mathcal{X}})}}\;\textnormal{err}{\left(\Phi;{\mathcal{F}}_{\epsilon}(P^{+})\right)}=\frac{s_{1}^{2}-(1-{\epsilon})^{2}}{s_{1}^{2}-s_{d+1}^{2}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmtheorem16.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem16.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Conversely, for any <math id="Thmtheorem16.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional encoder <math id="Thmtheorem16.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math> and any <math id="Thmtheorem16.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, there exists <math id="Thmtheorem16.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\in{L^{2}(P_{\mathcal{X}})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\in{L^{2}(P_{\mathcal{X}})}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="Thmtheorem16.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho(f,P^{+})=1-\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho(f,P^{+})=1-\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="Thmtheorem16.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textnormal{err}(\Phi,f)\geq\frac{s_{1}^{2}-(1-{\epsilon})^{2}}{s_{1}^{2}-s_{d+1}^{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext>err</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mfrac><mrow><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textnormal{err}(\Phi,f)\geq\frac{s_{1}^{2}-(1-{\epsilon})^{2}}{s_{1}^{2}-s_{d+1}^{2}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">This result has two parts.
First, we show that if <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> is compatible (<math id="S4.SS1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{*}\in{\mathcal{F}}_{\epsilon}(P^{+})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>ϵ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{*}\in{\mathcal{F}}_{\epsilon}(P^{+})</annotation></semantics></math>), the optimal encoder achieves low error on <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Second, we ask what if <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> is incompatible.
We cannot claim that no <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math> works for <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>—if one knows <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> a priori, then one can set <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{1}=f^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{1}=f^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> to achieve zero error.
Instead, we show that for any <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math>, there exists an <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> with the same compatibility as <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> for which <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math> performs poorly.
Therefore, compatibility reflects whether a context is suitable for a task.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Evaluating an arbitrary encoder.</h5>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The above result bounds the approximation error of the encoder that learns the contexture.
We can also bound the approximation error of an arbitrary encoder using the contexture theory.
See <a href="#A3" title="Appendix C Evaluating an Arbitrary Encoder ‣ Contextures: Representations from Contexts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>Implications for Scaling Laws</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Scaling laws <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx31" title="" class="ltx_ref">KMH+20</a>]</cite> state that the performance of deep neural models such as foundation models grow with their size.
Moreover, models of different architectures learn highly aligned <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx32" title="" class="ltx_ref">KNLH19</a>]</cite> representations when scaled up.
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx23" title="" class="ltx_ref">HCWI24</a>]</cite> thus proposed the platonic representation hypothesis that scaling makes representations more aligned with an underlying <span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">reality</span>, though they did not formally define this reality.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The contexture theory provides a new perspective on the role of scaling.
The function class from which the feature encoders <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math> are trained is a subset of <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{L^{2}(P_{\mathcal{X}})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{L^{2}(P_{\mathcal{X}})}</annotation></semantics></math>, and as the model gets larger, the function class approaches <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{L^{2}(P_{\mathcal{X}})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{L^{2}(P_{\mathcal{X}})}</annotation></semantics></math>.
This suggests that scaling brings the learned representation closer to the span of the top-<math id="S4.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> singular functions of <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{P^{+}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{P^{+}}</annotation></semantics></math>, explaining why big models learn aligned representations.
The key difference is that contexts are designed by humans and thus are more subjective than the so-called “underlying reality”.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We substantiate this extrapolation with an experiment on the <span id="S4.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">abalone</span> dataset from OpenML.
We use KNN (<math id="S4.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K=30" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>30</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K=30</annotation></semantics></math>) as context, where <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{A}}={\mathcal{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>=</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{A}}={\mathcal{X}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{+}</annotation></semantics></math> maps <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> to one of its <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> nearest neighbors equiprobably.
We compare two <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional representations with <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d=128" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>128</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=128</annotation></semantics></math> learned in the following two ways: (i) Kernel PCA to obtain the exact top-<math id="S4.SS2.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> eigenfunctions of <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{{k_{X}^{+}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{{k_{X}^{+}}}</annotation></semantics></math>; (ii) non-contrastive learning (<math id="S4.SS2.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{L}}_{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{L}}_{N}</annotation></semantics></math> in <a href="#Thmtheorem10" title="Theorem 10 (Proof in ). ‣ 3.2 Self-supervised Learning (SSL): Transformation Context ‣ 3 Learning the Contexture ‣ Contextures: Representations from Contexts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>) implemented with VICReg <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx11" title="" class="ltx_ref">BPL22</a>]</cite>.
For (ii), we use a fully-connected neural network with Tanh activation, skip connections, and AdamW optimizer <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx28" title="" class="ltx_ref">KB15</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx34" title="" class="ltx_ref">LH17</a>]</cite>.
We use the same number of training epochs for every model.
For each width and depth, we run the experiments 15 times with different random initializations, and report the average alignment.
See <a href="#A5" title="Appendix E Scaling Law Experiment Details ‣ Contextures: Representations from Contexts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E</span></a> for more details.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">We measure the correlation between the two representations using two metrics: the classical canonical-correlation analysis (CCA) metric <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{\rm{CCA}}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mi>CCA</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{\rm{CCA}}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, and the mutual KNN metric with 10 neighbors as used by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx23" title="" class="ltx_ref">HCWI24</a>]</cite>.
We center and whiten the representations (making the covariance identity) when using the mutual KNN.
CCA is invariant to all invertible linear transformations <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math>, which is ideal because such transformations do not affect the performance of the downstream linear probe, since one can adjust <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{W}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝑾</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{W}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{b}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒃</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{b}}</annotation></semantics></math> of the linear probe accordingly.
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx32" title="" class="ltx_ref">KNLH19</a>]</cite> also proposed the linear CKA metric, but we do not use it because it is only invariant to orthogonal transformations on <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F1" class="ltx_figure"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S4.F1.pic1" class="ltx_picture ltx_centering" height="433.43" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 550.9 433.43" width="550.9"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,433.43) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(51.29,0) translate(0,267.92)"><g transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -51.29 -41.74)"><g class="ltx_nestedsvg" transform="matrix(1 0 0 1 0 0) translate(51.29,0) translate(0,41.74)"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#808080;--ltx-fill-color:#808080;--ltx-fg-color:#808080;" color="#808080" fill="#808080" stroke="#808080" stroke-width="0.2pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M 0 0 L 0 5.91 M 32.73 0 L 32.73 5.91 M 65.46 0 L 65.46 5.91 M 98.18 0 L 98.18 5.91 M 130.91 0 L 130.91 5.91 M 163.64 0 L 163.64 5.91 M 196.37 0 L 196.37 5.91 M 229.09 0 L 229.09 5.91 M 261.82 0 L 261.82 5.91 M 294.55 0 L 294.55 5.91 M 327.28 0 L 327.28 5.91 M 360.01 0 L 360.01 5.91 M 392.73 0 L 392.73 5.91 M 0 165.23 L 0 159.33 M 32.73 165.23 L 32.73 159.33 M 65.46 165.23 L 65.46 159.33 M 98.18 165.23 L 98.18 159.33 M 130.91 165.23 L 130.91 159.33 M 163.64 165.23 L 163.64 159.33 M 196.37 165.23 L 196.37 159.33 M 229.09 165.23 L 229.09 159.33 M 261.82 165.23 L 261.82 159.33 M 294.55 165.23 L 294.55 159.33 M 327.28 165.23 L 327.28 159.33 M 360.01 165.23 L 360.01 159.33 M 392.73 165.23 L 392.73 159.33"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#808080;--ltx-fill-color:#808080;--ltx-fg-color:#808080;" color="#808080" fill="#808080" stroke="#808080" stroke-width="0.2pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M 0 0 L 5.91 0 M 0 51.64 L 5.91 51.64 M 0 103.27 L 5.91 103.27 M 0 154.91 L 5.91 154.91 M 392.73 0 L 386.83 0 M 392.73 51.64 L 386.83 51.64 M 392.73 103.27 L 386.83 103.27 M 392.73 154.91 L 386.83 154.91"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt"><path sty