<div id="S5.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Each dataset is randomly split into a pretrain set, a downstream labeled set, and a test set.
The downstream linear predictor is fit via ridge regression.
Hyperparameter grid search is conducted at both encoder learning and downstream stages.
The evaluation metric is the mean squared error.
Let <math id="S5.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textnormal{err}_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext>err</mtext><mi>d</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textnormal{err}_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> be the actual prediction error when <math id="S5.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S5.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional.
We test <math id="S5.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> up to <math id="S5.SS3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{0}=512" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>512</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{0}=512</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let <math id="S5.SS3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> be the one that minimizes <math id="S5.SS3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textnormal{err}_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext>err</mtext><mi>d</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textnormal{err}_{d}</annotation></semantics></math>.
We use the following four types of contexts.</p>
<ul id="S5.I3" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S5.I3.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I3.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I3.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">RBF kernels: <math id="S5.I3.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k(x,a)=\exp(-\gamma{\left\|x-a\right\|}^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k(x,a)=\exp(-\gamma{\left\|x-a\right\|}^{2})</annotation></semantics></math>. We define <math id="S5.I3.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{+}</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="S5.I3.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{+}{\left(a\;\middle|\;x\right)}\propto k(x,a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="true">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∝</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{+}{\left(a\;\middle|\;x\right)}\propto k(x,a)</annotation></semantics></math> for each <math id="S5.I3.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I3.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I3.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I3.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">KNN: <math id="S5.I3.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{+}{\left(a\;\middle|\;x\right)}=K^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="true">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{+}{\left(a\;\middle|\;x\right)}=K^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math> if <math id="S5.I3.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> is a KNN of <math id="S5.I3.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>, else 0.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I3.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I3.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I3.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">RBF<math id="S5.I3.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{}_{\textrm{mask}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext>mask</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{\textrm{mask}}</annotation></semantics></math>: First, randomly mask 20% of the features, and then apply RBF kernels to the other features. Specifically, we randomly draw 50 masks, and use the average of <math id="S5.I3.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{+}</annotation></semantics></math> over all masks as the context. We do not use masking alone because its association is too strong.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I3.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I3.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I3.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">KNN<math id="S5.I3.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{}_{\textrm{mask}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext>mask</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{\textrm{mask}}</annotation></semantics></math>: 20% random masking and then apply KNN.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="S5.SS3.p2.2" class="ltx_p">For each of these contexts, <math id="S5.SS3.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{A}}={\mathcal{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>=</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{A}}={\mathcal{X}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
For each type, we use 35 contexts by adjusting <math id="S5.SS3.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math> for RBF kernels and <math id="S5.SS3.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> for KNN. By doing so, we adjust the association level between <math id="S5.SS3.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS3.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>.
We make sure that contexts in every type range from very weak to very strong association.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In <a href="#S5.T1" title="In 5.3 Empirical Verification ‣ 5 Context Evaluation ‣ Contextures: Representations from Contexts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> we report the correlation between <math id="S5.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textnormal{err}_{d^{*}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext>err</mtext><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textnormal{err}_{d^{*}}</annotation></semantics></math> over all 140 contexts from the four types on 28 classification (Cls) and regression (Reg) datasets from OpenML <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx57" title="" class="ltx_ref">VvRBT13</a>]</cite> that are widely used in machine learning research.
The most common metric is the Pearson correlation, but it can only detect linear correlations, while the correlation between <math id="S5.SS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textnormal{err}_{d^{*}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext>err</mtext><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textnormal{err}_{d^{*}}</annotation></semantics></math> is not necessarily linear. Thus, we also report the distance correlation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx49" title="" class="ltx_ref">SRB07</a>]</cite>, another common metric that can detect non-linear correlations, but it cannot tell if the correlation is positive or negative because it is always non-negative.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The median reported in the table shows that on more than half of the datasets, the Pearson correlation is over <math id="S5.SS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.5</annotation></semantics></math>,
which is generally considered a strong correlation.
The distance correlation is even higher.
As expected, the metric does not work on all datasets.
For example, the Pearson correlation is very negative on <span id="S5.SS3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">brazilian_houses</span> and <span id="S5.SS3.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">fifa</span>.</p>
</div>
