<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="A4.T2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 2</span>: </span><span id="A4.T2.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Average estimation error of the top-<math id="A4.T2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="256" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>256</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">256</annotation></semantics></math> eigenvalues.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="A5" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix E </span>Scaling Law Experiment Details</h2>

<div id="A5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Here we provide a more detailed description of the experiment setting in <a href="#S4.SS2" title="4.2 Implications for Scaling Laws ‣ 4 Optimality of the Contexture ‣ Contextures: Representations from Contexts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<section id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Experiment overview.</h5>

<div id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The purpose of this experiment is to examine whether a large neural network can learn the contexture well, and whether scaling up the model size makes the learned representation more aligned to the top-<math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> eigenfunctions.
We compare two encoders.
The first encoder is obtained via kernel PCA on the dual kernel, so it consists of the exact top-<math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> eigenfunctions.
The second encoder is obtained via training a large neural network to optimize an objective that can learn the contexture.
Then, we compute the representational alignment of these two encoders.
The most classical metric is the canonical-correlation analysis (CCA) metric <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{\textrm{CCA}}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mtext>CCA</mtext><mn>2</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{\textrm{CCA}}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, which is invariant under invertible linear transformations to the encoders.
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx32" title="" class="ltx_ref">KNLH19</a>]</cite> proposed a variant called linear CKA, which is only invariant under orthogonal transformations.
In our setting, since we only care about the span of <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{1},\cdots,\phi_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{1},\cdots,\phi_{d}</annotation></semantics></math>, we would like the metric to be invariant under all invertible transformations, which is why we use CCA.
In addition, we also use the mutual KNN metric with 10 neighbors proposed by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx23" title="" class="ltx_ref">HCWI24</a>]</cite>, which measures the intersection over union (IoU) of nearest neighbors between the two representations.
This metric is not invariant under invertible linear transformations,
so we whiten the two representations such that their covariance matrices are both identities.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Setup.</h5>

<div id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We use the <span id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">abalone</span> dataset from OpenML, and split the dataset into a pretrain set, a downstream train set and a downstream test set by 70%-15%-15%.
We use K-nearest neighbors (KNN) with <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K=30" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>30</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K=30</annotation></semantics></math> as the context.
The embedding dimension is set to <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d=128" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>128</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=128</annotation></semantics></math>.
For the second encoder, we train a fully-connected neural network with Tanh activation and skip connections for a sufficient number of steps with full-batch AdamW, and vary the depth and width of the network so that we can study their effect on the alignment.
Here, “depth” refers to the number of hidden layers—for example, a 2-layer neural network has depth 1.
For each width and depth, we run the experiments 15 times with different random initializations and report the average alignment.</p>
</div>
<div id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In our experiments, we observe the <span id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">dimension collapse</span> problem <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx27" title="" class="ltx_ref">JVLT22</a>]</cite>—if we set the output dimension of the neural network to be <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>, then the rank of the learned representation will usually be less than <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>, meaning that it can only extract the top-<math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> eigenspace for some <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d^{\prime}&lt;d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d^{\prime}&lt;d</annotation></semantics></math>.
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx27" title="" class="ltx_ref">JVLT22</a>]</cite> proved that the training dynamics of self-supervised learning can cause this problem, that is, a large neural network trained with a gradient method cannot find the exact minima, but will find a low-rank solution instead.</p>
</div>
<div id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">To fix this issue, we set the output dimension of the neural network to be <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{1}=512&gt;d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>512</mn><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{1}=512&gt;d</annotation></semantics></math>.
After we obtain the <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional encoder, similar to <a href="#A4" title="Appendix D Efficient Estimation of the Context Usefulness Metric ‣ Contextures: Representations from Contexts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D</span></a> we estimate the matrices <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{C}}_{\Phi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝑪</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{C}}_{\Phi}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{B}}_{\Phi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝑩</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{B}}_{\Phi}</annotation></semantics></math>, and solve the generalized eigenvalue problem <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{B}}_{\Phi}{\bm{v}}=\lambda{\bm{C}}_{\Phi}{\bm{v}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝑩</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝒗</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝑪</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝒗</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{B}}_{\Phi}{\bm{v}}=\lambda{\bm{C}}_{\Phi}{\bm{v}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{V}}=[{\bm{v}}_{1},\cdots,{\bm{v}}_{d}]\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{1}\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝑽</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝒗</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒗</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{V}}=[{\bm{v}}_{1},\cdots,{\bm{v}}_{d}]\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{1}\times d}</annotation></semantics></math> be the top-<math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> eigenvectors;
then, we use <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\Phi}{\bm{V}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝑽</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\Phi}{\bm{V}}</annotation></semantics></math> as the <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional representation.
In other words, we use the 128 principal components of the 512-dimensional embedding.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A6" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix F </span>More on Context Evaluation</h2>

<div id="A6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we offer guidance for practitioners on identifying contexts with weak or strong associations with inputs. We then show that both excessively weak and overly strong associations degrade downstream performance and demonstrate that the proposed quantitative measurements accurately capture association strength in our controlled experiments. Moreover, we provide full experimental results that complements <a href="#S5.T1" title="In 5.3 Empirical Verification ‣ 5 Context Evaluation ‣ Contextures: Representations from Contexts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.
Finally, we provide proofs for the lemmas in Section <a href="#S5.SS1" title="5.1 The Effect of Context Association ‣ 5 Context Evaluation ‣ Contextures: Representations from Contexts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<section id="A6.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">F.1 </span>Quantitative measurements for level of association</h3>

<div id="A6.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A6.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">While mutual information captures mutual dependence between random variables, estimating it from samples remains a long-standing challenge as it requires the joint density function to be known <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx42" title="" class="ltx_ref">Pan03</a>]</cite>. To address this, we propose alternative metrics that are computationally more tractable.</p>
</div>
<div id="A6.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="A6.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A6.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A6.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A6.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A6.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Decay rate (all association):</span> As shown in the top row of <a href="#S5.F3" title="In Derivation of the metric. ‣ 5.2 Task-agnostic Evaluation of Contexts ‣ 5 Context Evaluation ‣ Contextures: Representations from Contexts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, the decay rate of singular values <math id="A6.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(s_{i})_{i\geq 0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(s_{i})_{i\geq 0}</annotation></semantics></math> reflects the strength of association. To estimate the decay rate <math id="A6.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>, we assume the singular values decay exponentially and fit the regression model <math id="A6.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{i}^{2}=\exp{(-\lambda i)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{i}^{2}=\exp{(-\lambda i)}</annotation></semantics></math>. When <math id="A6.I1.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> is large, it indicates a fast decay rate and context has low association. Conversely, when <math id="A6.I1.i1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> is low, it implies a slow decay and highly associated context.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A6.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A6.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A6.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A6.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Expected kernel deviation (weak association):</span> Since the kernel values <math id="A6.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{k_{A}^{+}}(a,a^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>A</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>a</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{k_{A}^{+}}(a,a^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> can be close to <math id="A6.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> for the contexts with low association, we propose using the expected absolute deviation from 1, i.e. <math id="A6.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}_{x,x^{\prime}\sim{P_{\mathcal{X}}}}\left[|{k_{X}^{+}}(x,x^{\prime})-1|\right]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}_{x,x^{\prime}\sim{P_{\mathcal{X}}}}\left[|{k_{X}^{+}}(x,x^{\prime})-1|\right]</annotation></semantics></math>, as a measure to indicate the weak association setting. Given samples <math id="A6.I1.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{1},\cdots,x_{n}\in{\mathcal{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{1},\cdots,x_{n}\in{\mathcal{X}}</annotation></semantics></math>, we use Monte Carlo sampling to approximate it, i.e.</p>
<table id="A6.Ex74" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A6.Ex74.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{1}{n^{2}}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\sum_{j=1}^{n}|{k_{X}^{+}}(x_{i},x_{j})-1|." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{1}{n^{2}}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\sum_{j=1}^{n}|{k_{X}^{+}}(x_{i},x_{j})-1|.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div></li>
<li id="A6.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A6.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A6.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A6.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lipschitz constant (strong association):</span> We empirically measure the Lipschitz constant of <math id="A6.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{k_{X}^{+}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{k_{X}^{+}}</annotation></semantics></math> for detecting contexts with strong association. Specifically, given samples <math id="A6.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{1},\cdots,x_{n}\in{\mathcal{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{1},\cdots,x_{n}\in{\mathcal{X}}</annotation></semantics></math>, we use the following estimation:</p>
<table id="A6.Ex75" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A6.Ex75.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{k_{X}^{+}}=\sup_{x,y,z\in{\mathcal{X}}}\frac{|{k_{X}^{+}}(z,x)-{k_{X}^{+}}(z,y)|}{||x-y||_{2}}\approx\max_{1\leq i&lt;j\leq n,1\leq k\leq n}\frac{|{k_{X}^{+}}(x_{k},x_{i})-{k_{X}^{+}}(x_{k},x_{j})|}{||x_{i}-x_{j}||_{2}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup></msub><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0.1389em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">sup</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow></mrow></munder><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub></mfrac></mrow><mo>≈</mo><mrow><munder><mi>max</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{k_{X}^{+}}=\sup_{x,y,z\in{\mathcal{X}}}\frac{|{k_{X}^{+}}(z,x)-{k_{X}^{+}}(z,y)|}{||x-y||_{2}}\approx\max_{1\leq i&lt;j\leq n,1\leq k\leq n}\frac{|{k_{X}^{+}}(x_{k},x_{i})-{k_{X}^{+}}(x_{k},x_{j})|}{||x_{i}-x_{j}||_{2}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="A6.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A6.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We note that the first metric requires estimating singular values <math id="A6.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(s_{i}^{2})_{i\geq 0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(s_{i}^{2})_{i\geq 0}</annotation></semantics></math> and the last two metrics rely on estimating kernel values <math id="A6.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{k_{X}^{+}}(x,x^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{k_{X}^{+}}(x,x^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math>. For estimating singular values, we employ the same technique as the task agnostic metric <math id="A6.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> in <a href="#S5.E4" title="In 5.2 Task-agnostic Evaluation of Contexts ‣ 5 Context Evaluation ‣ Contextures: Representations from Contexts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Equation</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>. Estimating kernel values, on the other hand, necessitates training an encoder <math id="A6.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math> and approximating <math id="A6.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{k_{X}^{+}}(x,x^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{k_{X}^{+}}(x,x^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="A6.SS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi(x)^{\top}\Phi(x^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi(x)^{\top}\Phi(x^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math>, which may require a large training set. Thus, decay rate measurements are preferred due to their simpler estimation process.</p>
</div>
<div id="A6.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A6.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Additionally, for the non-smooth kernel <math id="A6.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{k_{X}^{+}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{k_{X}^{+}}</annotation></semantics></math>, we have the following lemma showing that we may have the singular functions could be non-smooth and difficult to estimate.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem25" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem25.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 25</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem25.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Proof in <a href="#A6.SS3" title="F.3 Proof of Lemma ‣ Appendix F More on Context Evaluation ‣ Contextures: Representations from Contexts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">F.3</span></a>)<span id="Thmtheorem25.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem25.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem25.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem25.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let the Lipschitz constant for the positive pair kernel <math id="Thmtheorem25.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{k_{X}^{+}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{k_{X}^{+}}</annotation></semantics></math> be <math id="Thmtheorem25.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{k_{X}^{+}}=\sup_{x_{1},x_{2},x_{3}\in{\mathcal{X}}}\frac{|{k_{X}^{+}}(x_{3},x_{1})-{k_{X}^{+}}(x_{3},x_{2})|}{||x_{1}-x_{2}||_{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup></msub><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.167em">sup</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub></mfrac></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{k_{X}^{+}}=\sup_{x_{1},x_{2},x_{3}\in{\mathcal{X}}}\frac{|{k_{X}^{+}}(x_{3},x_{1})-{k_{X}^{+}}(x_{3},x_{2})|}{||x_{1}-x_{2}||_{2}}</annotation></semantics></math> and the maximum Lipschitz constant of its eigenfunctions be <math id="Thmtheorem25.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\mu}=\max_{i\geq 1}\sup_{x_{1},x_{2}\in{\mathcal{X}}}\frac{|\mu_{i}(x_{1})-\mu_{i}(x_{2})|}{||x_{1}-x_{2}||_{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>μ</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mmultiscripts><mo>sup</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow><mrow></mrow><mprescripts></mprescripts><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub></mfrac></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\mu}=\max_{i\geq 1}\sup_{x_{1},x_{2}\in{\mathcal{X}}}\frac{|\mu_{i}(x_{1})-\mu_{i}(x_{2})|}{||x_{1}-x_{2}||_{2}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Assume that all the eigenfunctions <math id="Thmtheorem25.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> are bounded by <math id="Thmtheorem25.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e. <math id="Thmtheorem25.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{i}(x)\leq c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{i}(x)\leq c</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="Thmtheorem25.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmtheorem25.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in{\mathcal{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in{\mathcal{X}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then we have <math id="Thmtheorem25.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{k_{X}^{+}}\leq cL_{\mu}\sum_{i=1}s_{i}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup></msub><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>μ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{k_{X}^{+}}\leq cL_{\mu}\sum_{i=1}s_{i}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A6.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A6.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Assume that there exists a universal <math id="A6.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> that bounds all eigenfunctions. For a highly non-smooth kernel <math id="A6.SS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{k_{X}^{+}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{k_{X}^{+}}</annotation></semantics></math> with high Lipschiz constant <math id="A6.SS1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{{k_{X}^{+}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{{k_{X}^{+}}}</annotation></semantics></math>, the lemma implies that we have either (i) smooth singular functions with large <math id="A6.SS1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\mu}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>μ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\mu}</annotation></semantics></math> and slow decay in singular values with small <math id="A6.SS1.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{i=1}s_{i}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{i=1}s_{i}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, or (ii) non-smooth singular functions with large <math id="A6.SS1.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\mu}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>μ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\mu}</annotation></semantics></math> and fast decay in singular values with small <math id="A6.SS1.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{i=1}s_{i}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{i=1}s_{i}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>.
For (i), we need a larger <math id="A6.SS1.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> to approximate the kernel well, which leads to a higher downstream sample complexity.
For (ii), the function approximation by neural networks becomes more difficult for non-smooth functions <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx59" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yar18</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<section id="A6.SS1.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">F.1.1 </span>Empirical Verification</h4>

<section id="A6.SS1.SSS1.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Setup.</h5>

<div id="A6.SS1.SSS1.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A6.SS1.SSS1.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We provide empirical evidence showing (1) downstream performance is worse for contexts with weak and strong associations, (2) the proposed quantitative measurements are positively correlated with the association level. To control the level of association, we use RBF kernels and KNN.</p>
</div>
<div id="A6.SS1.SSS1.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A6.SS1.SSS1.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For the estimation of kernel value <math id="A6.SS1.SSS1.Px1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{k_{X}^{+}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{k_{X}^{+}}</annotation></semantics></math>, we use <math id="A6.SS1.SSS1.Px1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{k_{X}^{+}}(x,x^{\prime})=\int\frac{P^{+}(a|x)P^{+}(a|x^{\prime})}{{P_{\mathcal{A}}}(a)}da" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{k_{X}^{+}}(x,x^{\prime})=\int\frac{P^{+}(a|x)P^{+}(a|x^{\prime})}{{P_{\mathcal{A}}}(a)}da</annotation></semantics></math> since <math id="A6.SS1.SSS1.Px1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{+}(a|x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{+}(a|x)</annotation></semantics></math> can be efficiently computed for these contexts. The decay rate is estimated using a non-linear least squares approach to fit the regression model. For computing the expected kernel deviation, we utilize the entire training set. To estimate the Lipschitz constant, we restrict the sample size to <math id="A6.SS1.SSS1.Px1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=1000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1000</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=1000</annotation></semantics></math> for computational efficiency.
Other experimental setups are the same as in <a href="#S5.SS3" title="5.3 Empirical Verification ‣ 5 Context Evaluation ‣ Contextures: Representations from Contexts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.3</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A6.SS1.SSS1.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Results.</h5>

<div id="A6.SS1.SSS1.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A6.SS1.SSS1.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><a href="#A6.F8" title="In F.3 Proof of Lemma ‣ Appendix F More on Context Evaluation ‣ Contextures: Representations from Contexts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a> and <a href="#A6.F9" title="In F.3 Proof of Lemma ‣ Appendix F More on Context Evaluation ‣ Contextures: Representations from Contexts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a> illustrate the relationship between association level and both the linear probe error <math id="A6.SS1.SSS1.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textnormal{err}_{d^{*}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext>err</mtext><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textnormal{err}_{d^{*}}</annotation></semantics></math> and the decay rate <math id="A6.SS1.SSS1.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> for KNN and RBF contexts, respectively. The results show that <math id="A6.SS1.SSS1.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textnormal{err}_{d^{*}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext>err</mtext><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textnormal{err}_{d^{*}}</annotation></semantics></math> increases at both extremes, with most blue curves exhibiting a U-shape. This suggests that both weak and strong association levels lead to higher errors. Additionally, the red curve indicates that the decay rate <math id="A6.SS1.SSS1.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> increases as the association level strengthens, highlighting a strong correlation between association level and spectral decay, which is effectively captured by the estimated decay rate.</p>
</div>
<div id="A6.SS1.SSS1.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A6.SS1.SSS1.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We report the relationship between association level and both the expected kernel deviation and the Lipschitz constant <math id="A6.SS1.SSS1.Px2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{{k_{X}^{+}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{{k_{X}^{+}}}</annotation></semantics></math> in <a href="#A6.F10" title="In F.3 Proof of Lemma ‣ Appendix F More on Context Evaluation ‣ Contextures: Representations from Contexts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a> for the KNN context and <a href="#A6.F11" title="In F.3 Proof of Lemma ‣ Appendix F More on Context Evaluation ‣ Contextures: Representations from Contexts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a> for the RBF context. The results show that contexts with low association exhibit small expected kernel deviations, while those with high association have large Lipschitz constants. These findings align with our theoretical developments in <a href="#S5.SS1" title="5.1 The Effect of Context Association ‣ 5 Context Evaluation ‣ Contextures: Representations from Contexts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A6.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">F.2 </span>Proof of Lemma <a href="#Thmtheorem17" title="Lemma 17 (Proof in ). ‣ Case 1: Weak association. ‣ 5.1 The Effect of Context Association ‣ 5 Context Evaluation ‣ Contextures: Representations from Contexts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">17</span></a></h3>

<div id="A6.SS2.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="A6.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A6.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A6.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text">Define <math id="A6.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{k_{X}^{+}}^{\prime}(x,x^{\prime})={k_{X}^{+}}(x,x^{\prime})-1=\sum_{i&gt;0}s_{i}^{2}\mu_{i}(x)\mu_{i}(x^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mmultiscripts><mi>k</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></mmultiscripts><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mrow><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{k_{X}^{+}}^{\prime}(x,x^{\prime})={k_{X}^{+}}(x,x^{\prime})-1=\sum_{i&gt;0}s_{i}^{2}\mu_{i}(x)\mu_{i}(x^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> that is the positive pair kernel without the trivial mode <math id="A6.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(s_{0},\mu_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(s_{0},\mu_{0})</annotation></semantics></math>, where the equality follows the definition of <math id="A6.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{{k_{X}^{+}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{{k_{X}^{+}}}</annotation></semantics></math>. We also denote the corresponding kernel integral operator as <math id="A6.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(T_{{k_{X}^{+}}^{\prime}}g)(x)=\int g(x^{\prime}){k_{X}^{+}}^{\prime}(x,x^{\prime})d{P_{\mathcal{X}}}(x^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mmultiscripts><mi>k</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></mmultiscripts></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mmultiscripts><mi>k</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></mmultiscripts><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(T_{{k_{X}^{+}}^{\prime}}g)(x)=\int g(x^{\prime}){k_{X}^{+}}^{\prime}(x,x^{\prime})d{P_{\mathcal{X}}}(x^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math>. Then we have</span></p>
<table id="A6.EGx8" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A6.Ex76"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A6.Ex76.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\sum_{i&gt;0}s_{i}^{2}=\Tr(T_{{k_{X}^{+}}^{\prime}})=\int{k_{X}^{+}}^{\prime}(x,x^{\prime})d{P_{\mathcal{X}}}(x^{\prime})&lt;\int\epsilon d{P_{\mathcal{X}}}(x^{\prime})=\epsilon." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></munder></mstyle><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>Tr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mmultiscripts><mi>k</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></mmultiscripts></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><mmultiscripts><mi>k</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></mmultiscripts><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mstyle><mo>&lt;</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mstyle><mo>=</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\sum_{i&gt;0}s_{i}^{2}=\Tr(T_{{k_{X}^{+}}^{\prime}})=\int{k_{X}^{+}}^{\prime}(x,x^{\prime})d{P_{\mathcal{X}}}(x^{\prime})&lt;\int\epsilon d{P_{\mathcal{X}}}(x^{\prime})=\epsilon.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A6.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A6.SS2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text">∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A6.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">F.3 </span>Proof of Lemma <a href="#Thmtheorem25" title="Lemma 25 (Proof in ). ‣ F.1 Quantitative measurements for level of association ‣ Appendix F More on Context Evaluation ‣ Contextures: Representations from Contexts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">25</span></a></h3>

<div id="A6.SS3.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="A6.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<table id="A6.EGx9" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A6.Ex77"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A6.Ex77.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle L_{k_{X}^{+}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle L_{k_{X}^{+}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A6.Ex77.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\sup_{x_{1},x_{2},x_{3}\in{\mathcal{X}}}\frac{|{k_{X}^{+}}(x_{3},x_{1})-{k_{X}^{+}}(x_{3},x_{2})|}{{\left\|x_{1}-x_{2}\right\|}_{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0.1389em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">sup</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow></mrow></munder><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\sup_{x_{1},x_{2},x_{3}\in{\mathcal{X}}}\frac{|{k_{X}^{+}}(x_{3},x_{1})-{k_{X}^{+}}(x_{3},x_{2})|}{{\left\|x_{1}-x_{2}\right\|}_{2}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A6.Ex78"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A6.Ex78.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\sup_{x_{1},x_{2},x_{3}\in{\mathcal{X}}}\frac{|\sum_{i\geq 0}s_{i}^{2}\mu_{i}(x_{3})\mu_{i}(x_{1})-\sum_{i\geq 0}s_{i}^{2}\mu_{i}(x_{3})\mu_{i}(x_{2})|}{{\left\|x_{1}-x_{2}\right\|}_{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0.1389em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">sup</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow></mrow></munder><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mrow><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">−</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mrow><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\sup_{x_{1},x_{2},x_{3}\in{\mathcal{X}}}\frac{|\sum_{i\geq 0}s_{i}^{2}\mu_{i}(x_{3})\mu_{i}(x_{1})-\sum_{i\geq 0}s_{i}^{2}\mu_{i}(x_{3})\mu_{i}(x_{2})|}{{\left\|x_{1}-x_{2}\right\|}_{2}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A6.Ex79"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A6.Ex79.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\sup_{x_{1},x_{2},x_{3}\in{\mathcal{X}}}\frac{|\sum_{i\geq 0}s_{i}^{2}\mu_{i}(x_{3})(\mu_{i}(x_{1})-\mu_{i}(x_{2})|}{{\left\|x_{1}-x_{2}\right\|}_{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0.1389em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">sup</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow></mrow></munder><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mo fence="false" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo>∑</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mi>s</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>μ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>μ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>μ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo fence="false" stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\sup_{x_{1},x_{2},x_{3}\in{\mathcal{X}}}\frac{|\sum_{i\geq 0}s_{i}^{2}\mu_{i}(x_{3})(\mu_{i}(x_{1})-\mu_{i}(x_{2})|}{{\left\|x_{1}-x_{2}\right\|}_{2}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A6.Ex80"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A6.Ex80.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle=\sup_{x_{1},x_{2},x_{3}\in{\mathcal{X}}}\frac{|\sum_{i&gt;0}s_{i}^{2}\mu_{i}(x_{3})(\mu_{i}(x_{1})-\mu_{i}(x_{2})|}{{\left\|x_{1}-x_{2}\right\|}_{2}}\ \ (\mu_{0}\equiv 1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><munder><mo lspace="0.1389em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">sup</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow></mrow></munder><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mo fence="false" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo>∑</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mi>s</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>μ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>μ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>μ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo fence="false" stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub></mfrac></mstyle><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>≡</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\sup_{x_{1},x_{2},x_{3}\in{\mathcal{X}}}\frac{|\sum_{i&gt;0}s_{i}^{2}\mu_{i}(x_{3})(\mu_{i}(x_{1})-\mu_{i}(x_{2})|}{{\left\|x_{1}-x_{2}\right\|}_{2}}\ \ (\mu_{0}\equiv 1)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
